CinefexSearchData.register("text/21",{"issue":21,"date":"April 1985","pages":[["1","Cover","number 21 $4.75 Canada - $5.95 cinefex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex CINEFEX 21 - April 1985. Published quarterly at P.O. Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $15; eight issues - $25; twelve issues - $35. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Sunnymead, California; color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1985 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor. Janine Pourroy contributing editors Adam Eisenberg Paul M. Sammon circulation manager Ann Dredla editorial assistant JoDyne Shay The Terminator When writer-director James Cameron first conceived of The Terminator, it was little more than a visceral image of a human cyborg emerging from a fire in its basic skeletal form. What it became was a modestlybudgeted blockbuster. To bring his image to life, Cameron engaged the services of Stan Winston - whose seasoned team of makeup and mechanical effects experts created the full-size robotic skeleton, as well as several lifelike representations of actor Arnold Schwarzenegger. For futuristic post-holocaust views of Los Angeles, plus contemporary pyrotechnics and stop-motion effects, the expertise and talents of Fantasy II were brought to bear. With minimal funds, but a wealth of creativity and enthusiasm, The Terminator's effects units helped transform Cameron's searing image into both a thrill-a-minute adventure and a major boxoffice event. Article by Jennifer Benidt. 24 The Shape of 'Dune' Despite enormous popularity as a novel, twenty years would elapse before Frank Herbert's Dune would make the quantum leap from printed page to cinematic reality. The imposing challenge of adapting the widely-read cult classic- a saga rivaling the novel itself in epic proportion -would ultimately be met by writer-director David Lynch. In consort with cinematographer Freddie Francis, production designer Tony Masters, and a battery of high-powered effects supervisors including Carlo Rambaldi, Albert Whitlock, Barry Nolan, Kit West and Brian Smithies, Lynch would labor diligently for three-and-a-half years to bring his vision of Herbert's exotic work to life. From the worm-infested deserts of Arrakis to the murky decadence of Giedi Prime, Lynch and his production unit combined a wealth of experience with fresh innovation to weave the richly-textured tapestry of Dune. Edited by Janine Pourroy and Don Shay. FRONT COVER - A cyborg endoskeleton emerges from the flames in The Terminator. A spice harvester scours the sands of Arrakis in Dune- BACK COVER."],["4","The Terminator","TERMINATOR"],["5","The Terminator","On the strength of its trailer, it looked like nothing more than the latest in a seemingly endless barrage of excessively violent action pictures, produced for short money and shamelessly offered up to a ravenous, but less-than-discriminating core audience weaned on mindless entertainment and cheap thrills. For those demanding only that, it was still an eminently satisfying film experience. But for others, The Terminator proved to be much more. Written with intelligence and executed with flair and precision, the film quickly became the surprise hit of the post-summer season, elbowing its way onto several 'Ten Best' lists for the year (including Time magazine's) and grossing more than $40 million for its modest $6.5 million investment. Written and directed by James Cameron and produced by Gale Anne Hurd - both latter-day alumni of Roger Corman's New World Pictures - The Terminator shifts back and forth between contemporary Los Angeles and the bombed-out ruins of the same city circa 2029. A maverick computerized defense system has wrested the future world from its creators, spawning an army of sentient machines bent on destroying the last remnants of humanity. Only a charismatic street fighter stands in their way. Under the rallying influence of John Connor, mankind is brought back from the brink of extinction, to the point of regaining its foothold on the planet, when the machine world reacts by sending its most formidable weapon -an indestructible cyborg Terminator (Arnold Schwarzenegger) - back to 1984 to assassinate the rebel leader's mother, Sarah Connor (Linda Hamilton), before her insurgent offspring is even conceived. To prevent this cataclysmic alteration of history, one of Connor's urban guerrillas, Kyle Reese (Michael Biehn), follows the implacable juggernaut back through time for an all-out confrontation in the streets of Los Angeles. \"I always conceive of a film as an image from which a story grows,\" reflected Cameron, whose only previous feature had been Piranha II, but whose prior experience had been in production design and visual effects photography on such ambitiously low-budget productions as Battle Beyond the Stars and Planet of Horror. \"In the case of The Terminator, the idea behind the image was a robot disguised as a human being. The robot goes through a tremendous fire and emerges from it as a combat endoskeleton. The image I started out with was of him stepping out of the fire - smoking, red-hot, metallic. I visualized him as skeletal, yet more massive than a skeleton. Then, to create the story, I worked backwards from that image and the desire to juxtapose that image with an everyday setting.\" Cameron's background in design and effects was to stand him in good stead, particularly during the early months of preproThe Terminator photographs © 1985 by Orion Pictures Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Joyce Rudolph, Additional photographs courtesy Joe Viskocil, Stan Winston, Doug Beswick and Ernie Farino. Special thanks to Gale Anne Hurd and Polly Apostolof. duction when he was writing, designing and storyboarding the film, and at the same time trying to assemble a team that could bring his ideas to life. For the all-important robot, plus a variety of prosthetic makeups, Cameron first contacted Rob Bottin, who in turn referred him to Stan Winston -a two-time Emmy winner for Gargoyles and The Autobiography of Miss Jane Pittman and an Oscar nominee for Heartbeeps, whose other credits include such widely divergent projects as The Wiz, W.C. Fields and Me, The Thingand Starman. Then, for miniature and optical effects required in the future sequences, plus a stopmotion version of the robot and a climactic tanker truck explosion, Cameron engaged the services of Fantasy II, a Burbankbased firm headed by Gene Warren Jr., and his partners Leslie Huntley and Peter Kleinow. Founded in 1980, Fantasy II had to their credit the Emmy Award-winning effects for The Winds of War miniseries, Goliath Awaits (also for TV) and Spacehunter. \"The design of the future sequences is very impressionistic and stroboscopic,\" Cameron explained, \"although I did do some research on the effects of nuclear weapons which I incorporated into my design. From a stylistic standpoint, I thought we'd have it be very monochromatic. Reese and the rebel fighters all wear camouflage which, in a burned concrete environment, would be dark gray. Their costumes are also adapted to the environment in that they have knee and elbow pads so that they can crawl put very lack o through the debris. We came up with a very black-and-white image, with all the color leeched out. The machines reflect that, too. They are chrome-plated, which gives you the sense that they are only and purely machines -with no human beings inside them - just because they look like metal. There's also a perfectly logical reason for the chrome plating, which is that a highly polished or mirrored surface will reflect a laser beam.\" To create an appropriate live-action setting for the futuristic sequences, Cameron and his crew secured permission to dynamite some condemned buildings at a Bethlehem Steel plant in nearby Vernon. From the debris, they created a mound of rubble two hundred feet long and eighty feet wide. Next, they brought in dump trucks full of wrecked cars and buses, fallen telephone poles and street lights, and piles of plaster human bones and skulls. The rubble heap was then backed by a flat plywood wall. \"I called it the Dresden Wall,\" Cameron commented. \"It was just a silhouette of ruined buildings, with a little impressionistic surface painting done on it. We introduced a lot of environmental smoke, and then backlit the wall so that it would only be a silhouette in the distance, glimpsed briefly when explosions go off and light it up.\" The ominous machines that roam the earth in Cameron's grim vision of the future were constructed solely as miniatures in the Fantasy II model shop. Working from the director's storyboards, Gene Warren and staff would study each effects shot and devise a means by which it could best be accomplished. Many were simple cutaways - straightforward inserts article by that could be achieved routinely with models and forced-perJennifer Benidt spective miniature landscapes. Others required closer interac-"],["6","The Terminator","A desperate battle for the place in contemporary Los Angeles in The Terminator, James Cameron. / By 2029, Los Angeles is little more than a sprawling plain of wipe out the last vestiges of machines, armed with highintensity searchlights and laser weapons. / Resistance fighters evade a Hunter-Killer tank - part of an elaborate tracking shot involving four setting. / From this grim Schwarzenegger) is sent into the past to assassinate the young woman who will give trail - a street-wise urban guerrilla, equally determined future of mankind takes written and directed by post-holocaust rubble - patrolled by sentient machines determined to humanity. Prowling the night skies overhead are flying Hunter-Killer separate live-action and miniature film elements rear-projected into a miniature foreground indestructible cyborg Terminator (Arnold birth to mankind's only hope for survival. On his future world, an to prevent it. 6 4 CINEFEX 21 tion between miniatures and live-action characters. Principal among the menacing machines were 'Hunter-Killer' tanks and aircraft. The 'hero' tank, four feet long and weighing two hundred pounds, was constructed by Fantasy Il model shop supervisor Michael Joyce. \"I built it the way we filmed it,\" said Joyce, \"from the bottom up. The first thing you see in the film is just the tread moving through the rubble. Then as Reese keeps flashing back, you see more and more of it. So I built the lower chassis and the wheels first. The bottom part of the tank was cast from a fiberglass, four-piece interlocking mold, and the chassis was aluminum, with all four of the treads on it. There were two 90-volt one-horsepower motors in each of the front treads, and 96 wheels on the tank, all spring-loaded so that when they ran over the debris it would work like a real tank. We built the main part out of foam and covered it with Presstex, which gives it a smooth finish. The upper part of the tank was radio-controlled. Its guns used four channels of the radio, and had axes that moved up and down and left and right. We had flashcubes inside the barrels to act as point sources for the lasers, which were added optically. The tank's head used three channels of another radio. It turned back and forth, and its searchlights were two 600-watt high-intensity quartz projector lights.\" In addition to the primary 'hero' tank, several undetailed spares were produced for the future flashback in which Reese and his commando team destroy one of the rumbling monsters. These and other miniature explosions in the film were engineered by veteran pyrotechnician Joe Viskocil. While Hunter-Killer tanks prowl the urban ruins ferreting out human victims, flying Hunter-Killers patrol the dark skies overhead. The basic shape of the three-foot flying machine, carved in foam and cast by modelmaker Gary Rhodaback, was detailed and outfitted by Paul Kassler with radio-controlled searchlights, running motors and landing gear. As with the tank shots, all the miniature flying scenes were created in heavy smoke environments using single-pass, non-motion control techniques. \"When the runs were straight,\" said Gene Warren, \"we had a tubular track that hung on the ceiling, with a carriage on it and another rig that came down so the model could be suspended on wires. For more elaborate kinds of moves, however, we used a Chapman crane with an operator sitting on top who could swivel and turn and tilt the model while it was arcing through the scene. Internal lighting proved to be a major difficulty, since we were shooting our high-speed miniatures 'live' on the set. Running lights and lights that blink or illuminate the interior are not generally a problem, although you do have to run them at a fairly high intensity. But creating searchlights that actually threw a beam through the smoke and lit up the ground was a real challenge because we were shooting high-speed with fairly small models, and so the light levels had to be very bright. What we ended up with were the same 600-watt quartz lights we had used on the tank, which were radio-controlled and employed dental lenses to focus the beam. The problem was they created lots of heat, so we had to run air in to them and they could be on"],["7","The Terminator","CINFFEY 21 • 7"],["8","The Terminator","8 4 CINEFEX 21"],["9","The Terminator","for only short periods of time or they would melt everything in sight. So whenever we did a shot, we got the cameras rolling and had everything up to speed, and then turned on the searchlights at the very last moment. Then we'd cut them off at the end, right away.\" Although straightforward wire work was used for the flying scenes, Warren and his unit were not above employing a few clever cheats. \"In the sequence where the ship's chasing the car,\" Warren explained, \"there are three or four cuts of it tracking along. For those, the ship was hung on wires off the Chapman crane, just as before, but it only moved in space about a foot or so. The camera was mounted on a dolly device - actually it was just sitting on foam rubber in a cart - and I moved it back and forth, over a very short distance, and tilted it as if we were tracking with it. What gave us the sense of speed was a fan blowing smoke past the ship, plus the fact that we had a moving background. All the buildings and ruins were mounted on two forty-foot tracks and we pulled them rapidly through the scene, with lots of flashbulbs mounted on the set and timed to suggest laser hits.\" The actual laser beams emanating from the Hunter-Killers were done using standard rotoscope techniques, but with a somewhat different approach. \"In science fiction movies,\" said Ernest D. Farino, in charge of graphic animation effects for the film, \"laser beams generally travel comparatively slowly from point to point so the viewer gets the full dramatic impact of the beam. It is technically a cheat, however, because light travels so rapidly that in reality it would be there for only an instant. To create a different look, Jim wanted the lasers to appear as complete beams on the first frame and then take three or four frames to fade out. \"Creating each beam's color, hot center and halo required three or four passes on the animation, which is not much except that in addition to all the matched passes being manually faded out on the animation stand, each pass had different time exposure settings - up to five seconds per frame per pass -and changes in f-stops, fog filters and plexiglass diffusion. It became a very laborious process. We also did a lot of camera move tracking, because many of the shots, both live-action and miniature, were moving camera shots. If a laser beam lasts six frames and the background is moving, the point from which each beam emanates has to be changed in every frame to follow the background image. And if the beam is traveling 'behind' other objects, each frame has to have a new overlay holdout matte cel. My assistant, Bret Mixon, was responsible for much of the rotoscope work and laser animation -and there were thousands of frames of it, especially in the car chase battle with the aerial H-K.\" Combining live-action and miniatures was another exercise in low-budget ingenuity. \"One of the reasons we were awarded the job,\" commented Warren, \"was that Jim Cameron wanted to avoid high-tech motion control firms that relied heavily on optically compositing a lot of elements. Although some shots are best accomplished by optical compositing, we shot in-camera as much as possible, using a combination of full miniatures and rear projection. One involved four pieces of film. It's a wide shot in the battle scene where Reese and the others come running in from the right and there's a big tank in back rolling along, and we truck along with them as they go behind a car and then stop and look at the explosion and then take off again. The stuff with the actors was done on two separate pieces of film - one for the right side and one for the left. I went out and shot those plates during the live-action shoot. The background was another two pieces of film. Then, on stage, we built a miniature set in front of a rear projection screen, with a ten-inch car and building pieces and other bits of junk that went right back to the screen, leaving just four small areas for the projections. Next we set up two rear projectors - one with the live-action footage where they come running in, and the other with the background tank material. With our tracking camera, we panned with them for a little bit and then trucked all the way across the set to the end of the shot. Once that was done, we backwound the film, returned the camera to its starting point, and repositioned the projectors with the second background piece and the live-action footage when they emerge from behind the car. Then we turned the foreground lights off and repeated the move, burning the second two elements into the scene. What we ended up with was a moving dolly shot with four elements. It worked pretty well.\" Warren was less impressed, however, with some additional rear-projected footage shot on stage with the actors. Though employing miniature plate material provided by Fantasy II, the live-action process work was done independently with an untested projector that produced both an unsteady image and a lighting imbalance between the live-action foreground and rear-projected plate. The Terminator and his pursuer arrive in present-day Los Angeles amidst a dazzling network of lightning bolts. Although most motion picture lightning is produced via cel animation for maximum control, the Terminator effects unit decided that real static discharges, generated with a Tesla coil and inserted optically, would be preferable in this application. \"The Tesla coil apparatus we used is now maintained and operated by Ed Angell,\" said Ernie Farino, \"but it was originally adapted for use in the movies back in the Thirties by Kenneth Strickfaden, who used it to produce lightning bolts in Frankenstein movies and the Flash Gordon serials. The technique is basically a matter of positioning two electrodes against a black background to create an arc of electricity. Gene Warren, Jim Cameron and I must have shot about a thousand feet of these lightning bolts. What I had done in advance was take the scenes we were dealing with, rotoscope them on the animation stand and make diagrams on cels of, for instance, the position of the garbage truck in the first sequence, to pinpoint the architectural details of each shot. While shooting, we physically hung those drawings in front of the camera so that we could position our electrodes to produce the lightning where we needed it for each shot. I later fine-tuned Hunter-Killer tanks and aircraft were strictly miniatures - built and photographed at Fantasy II. The primary tank - four feet long and weighing two hundred pounds - was constructed by Michael Joyce and equipped with motorized tank treads. / Effects supervisor Gene Warren photographs a closeup on the upper portion of the tank - which, for ease of operation, has been detached from its own chassis and mounted on a cart. Dense smoke environments were employed throughout to enhance aerial perspective. / Prior to smoking up the stage, Warren lines up a shot while Joyce operates the radio-controlled 'head' section and laser turrets. CINEFEX 21 • 9"],["10","The Terminator","mankind's cruel destiny line the war-torn streets of Los rumbling tank (not yet in ranging from half- to eighthpositioned only inches apart. Laser beams were inserted optically. / The aerial Hunterphysically drawn through a miniature representation of skeletonized skyscrapers. / constructed to scale for the tank shots. / Pyrotechnics expert Joe Viskocil prepares detailed copy of the primary Explosive charges destroy Grim reminders of Angeles. A high-speed miniature shot, this introduction to the film involved pyrotechnics, a view) and a forcedperspective foreground setup. The trailing skulls, scale, were actually Killer was suspended on wires - either from an overhead track or from a Chapman crane - and Street sections were to detonate a partially Hunter-Killer tank. / the tank. 10 • CINEFEX 21 that positioning - sometimes frame by frame - while compositing the shots on the optical printer. We also shot generic lightning bolts, which I later layered in separate passes on top of the other bolts to create a more frantic and exciting effect.\" The Terminator is a machine with a mission, dispassionately and methodically stamping out any and all obstacles in his path - from children's toys to nightclub bouncers to a fullymanned police station. Arriving unclothed and unarmed, he promptly rectifies both deficiencies by savaging a trio of punk hoodlums for their clothes and then relieving a gun shop proprietor of both his choicest weapons and his life. When injured, he discards the useless body part as perfunctorily as most people discard their trash. \"My initial intent was to do a film like Alien,\" commented Cameron, \"which, even though it's a science fiction film, was still done with a sense of very immediate, almost documentarylevel realism. The Terminator was going to be a guy with a scary face, but not particularly imposing. I intended for him to be kind of a lurking figure in the crowd, with the collar of his trench coat turned up like a bad guy from film noir. But once Arnold was cast, it changed the complexion of the film forever. It never would have worked to do that kind of gritty realism with Arnold in the role. He fills the space, and you have to go with that. You have to make it larger than life and play with it. If you think about it, Arnold really defies logic. He's supposed to be inconspicuous, an infiltrator. But at six-feet two-inches and 220 pounds, he stands out in any crowd. Plus he's got an accent, for which there's no logical justification, but people seem willing to suspend their disbelief. \"In retrospect, it seems strange that casting Arnold Schwarzenegger as the Terminator really hadn't occurred to anybody. His agent had originally wanted him to play Reese, which didn't strike me as appropriate casting. When I met Arnold, it turned out that he really loved the script. In fact, he was the only person I talked to before we started shooting who really believed that this was going to be a big movie. But interestingly enough, as we talked about the script, it turned out that a lot of the scenes he was responding to were Terminator scenes. Suddenly, it occurred to me that he'd make a great Terminatorl Arnold has a very powerful facial structure, as well as his physique. We never see his muscles, except in the first scenes, but we know they're there. We get a latent sense of his physical power, which pays off at the end when we see that he was strong even beyond the human strength. Also, if we had used another actor, we would have had to make the hydraulic endoskeleton that much smaller to fit inside him. With Arnold, you can buy the fact that his muscles would have concealed a lot of machinery.\" After an unabashed, but unsuccessful, attempt to assassinate Sarah Connor in a crowded nightclub, an ensuing car chase through the streets of Los Angeles ends with the Terminator's vehicle crashing headlong into an abutment. Escaping capture, but with severe eye and arm injuries, the cyborg repairs to a sleazy hotel room where he skillfully and painlessly performs"],["11","The Terminator","CINEFEX 21 • 11"],["12","The Terminator","corrective autosurgery. To achieve these scenes, Stan Winston and his crew were called upon to produce a lifelike prosthetic arm, plus a fully mechanized representation of Arnold Schwarzenegger's head. The first step in the process was to record the actor's facial features with a life cast. \"A life cast is only an impression,\" said Winston. \"It's not an exact likeness. When you're taking the impression, a certain amount of distortion inevitably takes place. The weight of the impression material pulls the face down, flattening out expression lines and removing the face's character. So when I make a duplication of an actor, I use the impression only as a starting point. I take the impression, then I make a clay duplicate of the life cast. I then take a series of photographs of very involved studies of the actor, in the expression he's going to be in for the shot. For example, if the expression in the shot is a blank one - as in the case of Arnold - then I take the photos of the actor with a blank stare on his face. If it's a scream, I will photograph the actor while he s screaming. I take these photos from every conceivable angle, and do very tight closeups of the eyes, the nose, the mouth. Once I have the photos, I then resculpt the entire face on the clay duplicate. Basically what I'm doing is sculpting the life and the character back into it. So the head I take my final cast from is actually a sculpture, not just a life cast. It looks exactly like the actor. \"To create the puppet heads, we make a silicone mold from the original sculpture. From that mold, we pull clay duplicates, which we then resculpt for each individual shot. After that sculpting is done, more silicone molds are made from those sculptures. We then lay out a thickness of clay inside our negative mold where we want the skin to be. On top of the clay layer, we lay in a core of Ultracal - which is a hard plaster. Next we remove the Ultracal and the clay, and then clean off the clay. The Ultracal core then fits back inside the silicone mold, leaving a void that's the thickness of the clay we laid out for the skin. We then inject foam rubber into that void to form the skin for the puppet head. Next, we make a fiberglass duplicate of the Ultracal core, which becomes the actual core that the puppet's skin fits on. We place whatever mechanics we need into that core - including teeth and a tongue - and then lay on the hair, imbed the eyelashes and do the final makeup and coloring.\" Two different puppet heads were required for the ocular surgery. \"The first head was sculpted with the eye and skin pulled open into the proper position. The reason for that is that rubber does not move exactly like real skin. Knowing that it would be very difficult to make a foam rubber eye and have it spread and do what a real eye would do, that head was sculpted with the pull already there, and thus has the correct dynamic look. The second head, which was revealed when the Terminator pulls the rag away from his eye, was sculpted to look as if that section of the eye had been cut away.\" For the last shot in the sequence - revealing the totally nonhuman core of the Terminator - Winston's crew developed an oversized, mechanical insert eye, constructed around a camera lens which irised open 12 < CINEFEX 21"],["13","The Terminator","and closed. For the Terminator's prosthetic arm, the makeup unit fabricated what was essentially a 'glove-arm' that a puppeteer could easily slide his hand in and out of - a technique Winston had employed once before on Dead and Buried. \"The thing that really helped us in the arm operation scene,\" Winston commented, \"was the fact that Arnold's hands are massive. The arm that we created is an exact duplicate of Arnold's, taken from an impression of his hand and arm in the proper position. We made a core inside the hand, which is hollow, and found a young girl - a flutist - whose hands were thin enough and whose fingers were long enough to fit up inside the hollow hand. The core was complex, made of three flexible inner cores of BJB 430 urethane. Sonny Burman and Bob Williams made the arm's inner working mechanical parts, then Shane Mahan dressed them with real meat and Hot-Melt veins and tissue. We used two arms - one for cutting and one for revealing the insides. The idea of the glove was very practical. The hand goes in, the hand comes out. There's no makeup or production time involved.\" The puppeteering often proved to be nearly as complicated as building the actual artifacts. During the arm operation, for instance, the puppeteer was crouched under the table in a very uncomfortable position, with her puppeting hand extending up through a hole in the table. For the eye operation, the hand holding the X-acto knife 'scalpel' was Schwarzenegger's, who was standing out of frame to one side of the head. Another weightlifter, with equally large hands, stood off to the other side and placed two of his fingers on the mechanical eye to give the illusion that he was propping it open. Two additional operators An even more complex puppet would be called for later in the film, when the Terminator -again pursuing Reese and Sarah Connor- is struck by a gasoline tanker truck and dragged beneath the heavy rig as it skids to a stop. In the process, much of the cyborg's facial flesh is torn away, revealing the mechanical skull underneath; but he still manages to pull himself from under the wheels, commandeer the vehicle and continue his relentless pursuit. For these scenes, a third puppet head was constructed - complete with multiple lacerations, a chromed plate under the forehead skin, an exposed mechanical eye and a large gaping hole in the cheek through which the jaw mechanisms could be seen. Winston also developed an elaborate matching makeup for Schwarzenegger, which worked so well that Cameron ended up shooting the puppet only for extreme closeups inside the truck. \"We used thin, vacuum-metalized plastic pieces that fit flush to Arnold's face,\" Winston explained. \"On top of those pieces, we used foam rubber appliances, which were sculpted to match the look of the puppeted head where the skin had been torn away. We then painted, in relief form, whatever mechanics we could onto that metal plate. For the red glowing eye effect, we took a little light bulb and a very thin red lens and laid them on top of the chrome covering that was over Arnold's eye, and then ran a wire over his ear and down his back. Jeff Dawn, John Rosengrant and Shane Mahan applied this makeup.\" The Terminator's point of view shots are among the film's. most subtle effects - yet they provide a chilling view of a world as envisioned through the unimpassioned eyes of a deadly machine. Dubbed 'Termovision' by the effects crew, the process was designed by Mark Sawicki and composited by Laurel Klick. \"It's basically a solarization type effect,\" explained Ernie Farino. \"We would take the footage that was shot on the set and then extrapolate that into high-contrast black-and-white images. By making negatives and positives at different exposures, the image would swell or shrink slightly. Then, by sandwiching these elements back together in certain combinations, we could isolate separate tonal qualities. We could then filter in any artificial color we wanted, but eventually settled on a pseudoinfrared look. Basically, this consisted of a combination of black, red and white tonal areas for the background, over which we burned in - on a separate exposure - the animated computer readout graphics. For the alley scenes, we added yet another element - an animated multiple-pass, hi-con overlay simulating a gun sight targeting circle.\" As the film progresses, the Terminator's human form is gradually stripped away, to the point of being reduced to a totally unadorned skeletal chassis when the tanker truck explodes and engulfs him in flame. The tanker explosion, one of the films most spectacular scenes, was actually shot in miniature at Fantasy II. Modelmakers Gary Rhodaback and Michael Joyce began by taking many photographs of the real tanker used during the live-action scenes. With these as reference, detailed blueprints were prepared, from which Rhodaback constructed an eight-foot-long miniature, built to one-sixth scale. All of the tanker parts were fashioned from scratch, cast from molds made by Rhodaback and Joyce. \"The mold for the tanker section was made from epoxy resins,\" Rhodaback explained. \"We made circles out of wood, and then strung them as if in the hull of a ship. We then put a piece of styrene all around that to make a solid male mold. To cast the tanker shell, we first laid in a wet polyester gel coat, then a layer of aluminum foil strips placed in a circular pattern, then another gel coat. The aluminum foil helped give the explosion scale. It also looked somewhat skeletal, as if the explosion had really shredded out the truck's steel layers. The shell itself was very fragile, about a thirty-secondth-of-an-inch thick. The rest of the truck's framing was plexiglass. Obviously, we couldn't use steel or any terribly solid objects which could fly from the explosion and possibly endanger a cameraman. The chassis was made from acrylics, the tires and wheels from urethane. The model was completely functional. It rolled, it turned, it had a very intricate wiring system -all its lights worked. Every detail on the full-size truck was reproduced on the miniature.\" The scene was shot in Fantasy Il's parking lot, with the live-action setting - the warehouse, the street, the parked car Since the basic image was being recorded on a single pass, high-intensity quartz lamps had to be built into the tank and flying miniatures in order to create searchlight beams that were strong enough to cut through the dense smoke levels maintained on the shooting stage. Laser beams - each requiring several artwork and diffusion passes - were subsequently rotoscoped into the shots by Ernest D. Farino and Bret Mixon. / An unwitting garbage collector on his nightly rounds is transfixed by the shower of lightning bolts which precedes the Terminator's arrival in time. The lightning effect was achieved by sandwiching and overlaying separately photographed static discharges generated by a Tesia coil. CINEFEX 21 • 13"],["14","The Terminator","Makeup artist Stan Winston puppetized representations of Arnold Schwarzenegger. duplicate of a life cast taken distorted by the impression Woodruff carves into the eye Terminator puppet awaits its final coloring and hair. / The articulated and controlled via cables. / The Terminator also repairs its damaged arm - a Schwarzenegger's. While the actor himself performed the beneath the table on which create convincing hand and and his crew were tasked with creating several Working with a clay directly from the actor, Winston carefully sculpts expression and character lines - often erased or material - back into the face. / Using Winston's 'corrected' life mask, Tom area to produce the basic sculpture needed for the puppet which performs ocular surgery on itself. / Nearly complete, the finished figure, fully duplicate of surgery, a hollow 'glove' hand - accessible from the arm was rested - enabled a young girl to finger movements. 14 • CINEFEX 21 - all duplicated in miniature. Joe Viskocil - whose pyrotechnic work has been featured in such productions as Star Wars, The Empire Strikes Back and Ghostbusters - was in charge of executing the blast. \"Once the model was finished, they turned it over to me, and I completely cut it up and scored it. For the explosives, I used a combination of small bombs and gasoline. I used forty-one separate charges that were all set off in exactly three-quarters of a second, real time. For dramatic impact, I wanted to go with a full gas tank type of explosion, but in miniature you have to break up the gas as much as possible. When you have a pure gas explosion, the initial flame is very long, so in miniature it would look out of scale. So I broke up the gasoline with sawdust and vermiculite. Then I needed to place the mixture in some sort of containers, and I found that condoms worked very well for that. I put about four ounces of the mixture into each condom, then placed them in plastic cups all over the sides of the tank. I covered the entire inner circumference of the tank with the explosives. We started the explosion in the back, rushing towards the cab of the truck since Reese puts the bomb into the truck's exhaust pipe. The live-action scene that immediately follows the explosion was actually shot before the explosion; and in that scene, the front of the cab is still standing. So I had to make sure that the cab was still standing after my explosion. And it was the only thing that was left The explosion was shot with three cameras - manned by Gene Warren, Peter Kleinow and John Huneck - all running at 120 frames per second. Unfortunately, the first attempt failed. Since the tanker was being photographed at high-speed, it had to be moving rapidly to compensate for the slow motion effect. The vehicle was designed to be cable-drawn by a motorized drive unit positioned out of frame; but with the added weight of the explosives in the tank section, the connection between the cab and tank was not strong enough to withstand the initial jerk required to get it rolling. As a result, the truck's front axle came off. The bombs detonated as planned, but the truck's wheels were already out in front. So the tanker had to be rebuilt virtually from scratch. What had originally taken two months to set up had to be redone in less than a week. Rhodaback and Joyce were able to salvage most of the cab, and miraculously reconstructed the entire truck in only three days. The vehicle was then rerigged with explosives and detonated a second time - with perfect results. Just as Reese and Sarah begin to believe they have at last stopped the unstoppable, out of the blazing inferno steps the Terminator endoskeleton - Jim Cameron's visual inspiration for the film brought bodily to life - stripped of all human semblance and ready for battle in its most basic form. \"Stan Winston had always wanted, as had I, to do the definitive movie robot,\" said Cameron. \"We both knew from the start that it would have to be some kind of puppet. There was no way we could do the robot as a man in a suit. It's supposed to be a skeleton, so you have to be able to see through him in places. I"],["15","The Terminator","CINEFEX 21 • 15"],["16","The Terminator","16 4 CINEFEX 21"],["17","The Terminator","knew, though, that it would have to be an incredibly complicated and detailed puppet. We had to be able to see more of it than, say, the alien in Alien. We couldn't just hide it in shadows and see it in quick cuts. There had to be enough of it on the screen to retain the massive terrifying image that the Terminator had been throughout the movie.\" Although it was Cameron's intent to give Winston creative license in designing the robot, he nevertheless submitted some of his own sketches as points of departure. Winston, however, was so taken with Cameron's concepts that he had no desire to alter them. \"Jim happens to be an incredible artist,\" Winston explained, \"which was great for me because most of the time my biggest problem is working with directors who don't have an artistic concept. Usually, I do all my own design work; but in this case there was already a concept that was brilliant, and I didn't want to change it.\" At Cameron's insistence, however, Winston came up with three variations on the basic theme. In the end, the design reverted back to essentially what the director had at first proposed. The only modification of note is that the final robot is slightly larger and more massive than originally conceived - due in part to the casting of Arnold Schwarzenegger after the film was already in preproduction. \"I wanted to retain Arnold's form in building the robot,\" said Winston. \"Not only is the robot the same height as Arnold, but all of its proportions are scaled down from and matched to fit his. The robot is anatomically correct, and could literally fit inside Arnold's body. Even the robot's skull was scaled down from a clay duplicate of Arnold's head; and its teeth are duplicates of Arnold's. However, since the skull we created was a mechanical one, it is not exactly like a human skull. We had to use some artistic license. Jim's concept of the robot was that it be organic, yet look like a machine \" Constructing the robot was a laborious process taking about six months from start to finish. Winston's multi-skilled crew - consisting of Shane Mahan, Tom Woodruff, John Rosengrant, Richard Landon, Brian Wade, David Miller and Michael Mills - handled the sculpting, moldmaking and even puppeteering, while the machining, metal work and radio control were accomplished by Ellis 'Sonny' Burman, Bob Williams and assistant Ron MacInnes. \"Each of the robot parts was originally sculpted in Roma clay,\" Winston explained, \"then molded in gray tuffy, which is a polysulfide flexible rubber compound. Plaster copies were then brought out of those molds. The finished work was done on those plaster castings, which were finely sanded to give them a machined look. Then the plaster castings were molded in BJB 430 urethane, with the final epoxy castings being brought out of those urethane molds. The composition of the epoxy castings was, first, an initial coat of epoxy, then a fiberglass cloth coat, then another layer of epoxy. To give the castings strength, we embedded custom-fit steel ribbing into them, and filled them solid with epoxy. The final pieces were then finely sanded, vacuum metalized, and painted down to give them a distressed look.\" Modelmaker Gary Rhodaback adds finishing detail to a sixth-scale tanker truck constructed for the film's fiery climax. Once completed, Joe Viskocil dismantled the miniature, loaded it with gasoline and explosive charges, and then reassembled it. / Outside, in the Fantasy II parking lot, a similarly scaled reproduction of the warehouse and street location used during the live-action photography was erected. A concealed, cabledrawn mechanism propelled the truck through the miniature setting. / Viskocil's timed explosion - taking three-quarters of a second for full envelopment - was photographed at high-speed from three different camera positions. CINEFEX 21 • 17"],["18","The Terminator","closeups in the truck cab. The Terminator, struck by the tanker and dragged along severely damaged and much of his facial tissue has been torn away. Stan Winston and articulated figure complete with multiple lacerations, a chromed forehead plate, an exposed eye mechanism and a gaping hole in the cheek. / For scenes where use of the corresponding makeup that could wear - comprised of appliances painted in relief. / conflagration fails to stop stripped entirely of flesh and Another Schwarzenegger puppet was required for the pavement, is now crew prepared a final puppet was infeasible, Winston designed a Schwarzenegger himself plastic and foam rubber the Terminator. Within moments, the cyborg emerges from the flames, reduced to its most basic Even the tanker skeletal form. 18 4 CINEFEX 21 Because of the steel ribbing that was added to each of the robot's pieces, the Terminator puppet turned out to be much heavier than originally anticipated. Although the added weight caused problems in puppeteering the robot, it proved beneficial by helping to create a more solid image for the screen. \"One of the reasons that the robot looked so real and seemed so massive was that heavy. His weight generated onto the screen and made him appear more powerful. When he walked, those were real metal feet hitting the floor.\" Puppeteering the man-size, hundred-pound robot was no easy task. Winston and his crew devised several different puppeting techniques -which varied from shot to shot since each presented its own unique set of problems. The robot's introductory scene, rising from the burning rubble behind Reese and Sarah, was accomplished with a fulcrum device and a half-dozen operators. For above-the-waist shots of the Terminator walking, the robot's torso was actually propped up on Shane Mahan's back, supported in a rig similar to a backpack. \"Since the robot's leg was damaged,\" Winston explained, \"Shane would walk with a limp and his movements would generate up through the puppet. That made it move more organically, and less like a puppet bouncing up and down.\" Mahan would move the arms, as well, while Bob Williams operated the head via radio control. \"Radio control has been criticized for not looking as fluid or organic as puppetry,\" Winston continued. \"However, in this case, it worked out beautifully, because we were controlling a robot, so any mechanical movements actually helped us.\" For closeups requiring rapid or extreme movements, the robot head was detached from the body and the radio control mechanism removed, therefore providing ample room for Tom Woodruff to insert his hand and puppeteer the head manually. The puppet's limbs could also be detached when necessary, as with the inserts of the robot's feet - achieved with two puppeteers, each When Reese and Sarah take refuge in a factory -actually a Los Angeles canning plant - the script called for the robot to beat the door down after them. \"For those shots,\" said Winston, \"we built an actual battering ram - a metal plate that was fitted around the outside of the puppet's arm and went through its body, so that it could knock the door down itself. The robot was on John's and Tom's backs, Shane and Richard were each on an arm, and I was the battering ram. The whole rig was on a rolling trolley, and we would actually shove the robot right into the door. We purposely withheld that shot to the end of the production because we didn't know how much of the robot would be left afterwards -but he withstood it completely.\" Jim Cameron was more than impressed, both with the robot's overall performance and with its durability. \"We had no time to test the puppet to see if it was going to work, and I must say that it performed perfectly. It survived two months of shooting with no major flaws that couldn't be repaired between shots. It was an amazing piece of work.\" Although the full-size puppet was employed for scenes featuring various parts of the robot's anatomy -or for head-to-toe shots whenever it was in a stationary position -any shot involving the full robot walking required stop-motion animation. Cameron knew from the start that he would have to use stopmotion for some of the robot scenes; and though generally wary of the technique, he reasoned that it would be altogether appropriate for the robot Terminator. \"I feel that stop-motion works well when you're animating a machine. I've never really bought the reality level of living organisms being animated. Human beings are the result of two million years of evolution. We know how another person feels and thinks just by how he walks and stands. But we don't know how a robot moves, so that immediately throws the rule book out the window. My justification for using stop-motion for the robot was that it's a machine, and the eye can accept a certain difference of motion.\" In order to minimize continuity problems with sequences involving both robots, the stop-motion puppet had to look as much like the full-size puppet as possible. With this in mind, Gene Warren hired Doug Beswick to construct the two-foot-tall replica. \"Because of scheduling,\" said Beswick, \"we built our small puppet at the same time the big one was being built. We worked very closely with Stan Winston. As soon as he completed each piece, we would copy it exactly, only on a scale one-third the size. We did that by measuring the full-size part and then doing our own detailed drawings to a one-third scale dimension. From these drawings, we built our miniature pieces. The basic armature was machined out of aluminum -except for the fingers, which were brass, and the feet. The feet were made out of steel, for extra strength. Since the ankle joints had to support a two-foot-tall puppet - which is a pretty good size - and since they were human-proportioned and thus very small in relation to the rest of the figure, they had to be the strongest joints in the puppet.\" Beswick himself designed all the miniature joints, and did the necessary machining for the figure. But not all of the puppet could be fully machined. Certain portions were sculpted by Willy Whitten. \"Since we were dealing with machine parts,\" Beswick continued, \"some of the minute radiuses and contours we just couldn't duplicate in miniature. When that happened, Willy would apply auto body filler right onto the metal part and then sand it and carve it and do whatever he had to do to make it match Stan's. Other areas - such as the skull, chest, vertebrae and pelvis - had to be totally sculpted and cast. In those cases, Willy would apply Sculpey -which is a modeling compound that can be hardened by baking -right over my armature pieces and do his sculpting right there. We would then make a mold from that, using the armature piece as a keyed section. Later, after the mold was completed, we could take the Sculpey off the armature, set the armature piece back in the mold, and then run epoxy mixed with aluminum powder and make the actual cast part that way. We put aluminum in the epoxy to give it a metallic look, even though the puppet was going to be"],["19","The Terminator","CINEFEX 21 • 19"],["20","The Terminator","20 4 CINEFEX 21"],["21","The Terminator","chrome-plated later on. My theory was, if I did everything pretty much like Stan did, our finish would resemble his. So we used the same epoxy, and the same ratio of aluminum to epoxy as he did. We even went to the same plater he did in order to get the same tones and everything. \"When the pieces came back from the plater -and there were hundreds of them - Ted Rae spent about two full weeks assembling the model, doing the detail work as he went along. Things like hydraulic hoses had to be added and we even attempted to match the nuts and bolts sizes, scaling down what Stan had used to some smaller sizes that were available - and what weren't, we made. So everything worked. Nothing seemed oversized. Mark Sullivan then painted the chrome finish to give it the same scorched look the larger robot had.\" The final assignment ended up taking five months to complete - two months longer than Beswick had anticipated. \"I feel it was worth the extra effort, though. The end result is what everybody remembers, and that robot segment was very important to the Animating the puppet was no small task. \"Working with a puppet that size is unusual,\" said Peter Kleinow, Fantasy Il's resident stop-motion practitioner. \"Normally, the average size for such a puppet would be one foot, but Doug had to make it larger because the detail on the full-scale puppet was so great that we couldn't go any smaller and still have working detail. The difficulty in animating a larger puppet is a question of using reference points. When you're animating, you set up a reference gauge over the puppet so that you know you're always moving it in the same direction. If you have to set up a whole bunch of reference points, it makes it much more complicated. On a big puppet, you can't get by with just one reference point - you need two or three gauges.\" Kleinow experimented with different techniques to try to inject as much realism as possible into his shots. \"Usually, stop-motion is shot with the camera locked off in a stationary position, which tends to subliminally draw the viewer's attention to the fact that it's not real. To counter this, we added little camera moves to the shots, just like a live-action cameraman does. It adds a subliminal sense of reality. In a couple of those shots, a slight pan was used. Since we were filming a combination of puppet, rear projection and foreground elements, we used a nodal point pan so that everything stayed together and didn't shift. Another way we tried to add some realism was by blurring the Terminator's movements. A lot of his movements are very rapid, so we would add blurs, just like a live-action film is blurred. We did that by putting a piece of glass between the camera and the puppet and blurring the image by smearing Vaseline on the glass. We'd have to stop after each frame was exposed, clean the glass, and then blur it again from a different position.\" Animating a stop-motion puppet is always a time-consuming task, and the Terminator robot proved to be no exception. \"For every stop-motion shot we wound up using,\" Cameron comDesigned by James Cameron - with only a few essential modifications by Stan Winston - the full-size endoskeleton was pieced together primarily from epoxy castings reinforced with steel ribbing. Shane Mahan surveys his work on the first-stage sculpting of the robot skull. / Bob Williams installs a radio control mechanism used to articulate the head. For closeups requiring rapid or extreme movements, the head could also be puppeteered from within, by hand. / Once the pieces were completed, each was sent out for chrome plating. The full figure was then assembled in Winston's shop. / Various techniques were used to mobilize the robot. For waist-up shots, the upper body section was fitted onto a backpack rig worn by Mahan. CINEFEX 21 • 21"],["22","The Terminator","For full-figure walking shots, a two-foot-tall stop-motion robot - matched precisely to its full-scale counterpartwas constructed by Doug Beswick. / Ted Rae prepares the puppet for a stop-motion scene - eventually cut from destroyed by a pipe bomb, continuing in relentless pursuit of its quarry. / The stop-motion Terminator through a hallway in the factory. / Animator Peter large-scale puppet between exposures. Background plate projected, frame per frame. / Another stop-motion setup inside the factory. Whenever possible, camera moves and introduced to help make the the film - showing the Terminator, partially robot stalks its victims Kleinow repositions the photography is rearmotion blurs were animation more lifelike and convincing. 22 4 CINEFEX 21 mented, \"Pete did anywhere from two to five takes. When you multiply that out by the time it takes to do a single take - which is somewhere between six and sixteen hours - we're talking about a lot of work for one person. We had originally intended to do twelve to fourteen stop-motion shots. There are eight in the film, and I feel we came out even. Pete did a great job with them.\" The beginning of the end for the Terminator occurs in the factory as the towering robot looms over his formidable, though all-but-defeated adversary. With a last burst of adrenalized strength, Reese wedges a pipe bomb into the cyborg's rib cage - and before the robot can extract it, it explodes. \"For that shot,\" said Winston, \"we constructed a rigid fiberglass replica of our robot, positioned it over Reese, and then just blew it up.\" Even that, however, does not stop the Terminator. Devoid of its legs and one of its hands, the relentless machine still continues in its pursuit of Sarah, dragging itself across the latticework floor. \"Jim and I had discussions during preproduction about designing certain sets in such a way that they would allow us to put rods up through the floor to move our robot. That grating on the factory floor was there specifically to help us with the movement at that point. For those crawling shots, we cocked the puppet's head backward and operated both the head and eyes by radio control.\" Scrambling away from the ever-advancing robot, Sarah crawls through a giant steel press, lures the robot in after her, and then slithers out and trips the switch just as the robot's hand closes around her throat. Richard Landon constructed a special robot for the Terminator's final termination. \"We cast all the parts in BJB 430 polyurethane,\" Winston explained, \"so that it would collapse rather than snap under the crusher. Since the Terminator is a metal robot, we needed to use a material that would crush like metal would. If we had made it in a fiberglass, it would have broken like glass. So we cast him out of urethane, vacuum metalized him, set him up in his crawling position, and then crushed him.\" And thus was James Cameron's cybernetic juggernaut at last dispatched - finally and completely, but perhaps not permanently. Spurred on by the phenomenal success of their maiden venture, Hurd and Cameron are already discussing a sequel. Meanwhile, however, other projects are in development, including Cameron's long-planned follow-up to Alien -with a possible reunion of the effects team that brought such an abundance of resourcefulness and dedication to The Terminator. \"I think the reason people were so excited about working on The Terminator,\" Cameron concluded, \"was that they were initially impressed by the script. Often people involved in the type of work done by Stan Winston and Fantasy Il find themselves doing a lot of bad 'blood-and-guts' movies just to bring in revenue and survive. So when they get a chance to work on a movie with a good script, they are willing to work that much harder to produce something they can be proud of and that people will enjoy. Whatever the reason, everyone involved with The Terminator did a terrific job.\""],["23","The Terminator","CINEFEX 21 • 23"],["24","The Shape of ‘Dune’","THE SHAPE OF DUNE"],["25","The Shape of ‘Dune’","From its first installment, seasoned readers of Analog magazine knew they were experiencing no ordinary science fiction yarn. Frank herbert's Dune World - an epic tapestry of survival and adventure, intricately woven and with an astonishing array of characters and settings - was clearly something special. Yet even with its book publication in 1965, Dune did not exactly set the literary world on fire. \"Initially,\" Frank Herbert recalled, iDune was turned down by more publishers than I can even remember. Then when Chilton finally did bring out the book, those reviewers who even bothered to write about it were overwhelmingly negative. When the book hit - and it hit big, so big that the publishers couldn't keep up with the demand - it was mostly via word of mouth.\" Although best known for his science fiction novels, Frank Herbert is a man of diverse talents, having worked at various points in his life as a jungle survival instructor, journalist, television cameraman, radio news commentator, oyster diver and lay analyst. His equally diverse interests include oriental philosophy, undersea geology, anthropology, electronics - and perhaps most significantly - ecology. For it was Herbert's passionate regard for ecology which led to his eventual penning of Dune. \"During the early 1960s, I was in Florence, Oregon, to do an article about the Agricultural Department's work on a number of their projects centering around sand dunes. They had assembled experts from all over the world to pool their knowledge about controlling dunes, because they were worried about the fact that when sand dunes decide to move, they move. They can completely overrun homes, plantations and just about anything else. So, intrigued by this idea of controlling sand movement, I started doing my own research into the area. Around this time, I had also been doing a hell of a lot of research for a book I was tinkering with concerning the messianic impulse and why people have a tendency to follow charismatic leaders without question -which I think is a very, very dangerous thing. So one thing led to another, and I combined dune research with this anti-messianic idea, and after six years of research and a year-and-a-half of writing, Dune was born.\" In short order, Dune became a cult classic - the science fiction novel, along with Robert A. Heinlein's Stranger in a Strange Land, that was read and passed around all the major college campuses then embroiled with the pro-arts, pro-revolution radical Sixties. From there, it broke loose from the student ghettos and overflowed into the literary mainstream to the point where Dune and its sequels have by now sold more than Dune photographs copyright ® 1984 by Universal City Studios and Dino De Laurentiis Corporation. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by George Whitear. Effects unit still photography by Eric Swenson. Additional photographs courtesy of Carlo Rambaldi and Danielle Verse. This article has been compiled largely from interviews and insights provided by Paul M. Sammon and his video featurette Destination Dune. ten million copies in fourteen languages. Curiously, however, more than two decades would pass before writer-director David Lynch and executive producer Dino De Laurentiis would bring this richly textured work to the screen. With a $50 million-plus production tab, and a $26 million advertising budget, Frank Herbert's vision of the vast desert planet Arrakis - a world of secretive nomadic tribes, mile-long sandworms and the lifeprolonging, consciousness-raising spice melange - would become a motion picture reality. It was not for lack of interest, however, that Dune found itself in a twenty-year holding pattern from page to screen. In 1972, producer Arthur P. Jacobs - riding high on his Planet of the Apes series - purchased a nine-year option on the book, which he hoped eventually to film in Turkey. Herbert himself was to have been the technical advisor on the project, but the film was still in treatment stage when Jacobs was felled by a fatal heart attack in 1973. Not long after Jacobs' death, Michael Seydoux, a wealthy Parisian eager to break into the film business, bought the rights to Dune. For his director and screenwriter, Seydoux tapped Chilean-born Alexandro Jodorowsky, whose violent allegorical western, El Topo, had taken the midnight movie circuit by storm. At Jodorowsky's insistence, an intensive preproduction effort was launched - a phase which would ultimately drag on for nearly two years. Though it was Jodorowsky's intent to stress the novel's mystical and religious elements, he was equally determined to pursue a richly varied visual style. Predating such 'designer cinema' projects as Star Wars, Alien and Conan the Barbarian, Jodorowsky's Dune was to be visualized by a number of artists, each with a distinct style and each with a separate responsibility for designing one of the four main planets of the book. First on the list was French comic book artist Jean 'Moebius' Giraud, who assisted Jodorowsky throughout by creating more than a thousand pages of extremely elaborate storyboards and designs. Others included British illustrator Chris Foss, Swiss surrealist H.R. Giger, American comic book artist Richard Corben and American artist Ron Cobb. Also of prime concern were the special effects. After first approaching Douglas Trumbull, Jodorowsky turned to Dan O'Bannon, whose ultra-low-budget work on Dark Starhe had found impressive. In short order, O'Bannon found himself head of the Dune special effects unit, spending six preparatory months in Paris with a crew of thirty. It was at this point - with sets and props designed and ready to be built - that Jodorowsky's Dune went belly-up. Having already expended a third of their initial $6 million budget, Jodorowsky and Seydoux were unable to secure the additional funding needed, and the project collapsed. One person not at all surprised by this turn of events was Frank Herbert. \"Without exaggeration, Jodorowsky's Dune would have made an elevenor twelve-hour movie. His script was the size of a telephone book. And he'd made many changes.\" Among Jodorowsky's alterations was the climactic death of Paul Atreides, a death which turned the planet Arrakis - better known as Dune - into edited by Janine Pourroy & Don Shay"],["26","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Writer-director David Lynch surveys the action during Dune location shooting in Mexico's Samalayuca Desert. The site - deemed by Lynch to be an aesthetically perfect representation of the planet Arrakis - was used for only photography taking place on Churubusco in Mexico City. Herbert's internationally acclaimed science fiction novel would be three-and-ahalf years in the making, four weeks, with the majority of principal the soundstages and backlots of the Estudios The Dino De Laurentiis production of Frank at a cost of over $50 million. 26 • CINEFEX 21 a living, intelligent planet, one which gives birth to a thinking galaxy that, in turn, transforms the entire universe into a benevolent communal mind sympathetically 'fused' with all of humanity. \"After the failure of Jodorowsky's Dune, I thought to myself, 'Well, that's it Frank; nobody's ever going to crack this book.\" herbert, of course, would ultimately be proven wrong - but not before a third abortive attempt was undertaken. In 1976, Italian producer Dino De Laurentiis stepped in, buying the Dune rights from Seydoux. Although well aware of the book's troubled past, De Laurentiis remained unfazed by the risks and expense involved, and spent yet another six years developing the project. A man with more than 600 production credits to his name and 35 years in the business, De Laurentiis decided to return to the source by commissioning a script from none other than Frank Herbert. But Herbert's resulting 175page screenplay was deemed unsatisfactory-\"I guess I was just too close to the book to do the necessary paring down\" - and, after reading the novel three times, De Laurentiis decided to first hire a director with a strong visual sense and then find a workable script. So late in 1979, director Ridley Scott - a hot property due to his then recently-released Alien - was hired by De Laurentiis to helm the third attempt at a Dune film. Working out of London's Pinewood Studios, Scott hired H.R. Giger as his production designer, and began to translate Herbert's novel into a series of storyboards and other drawings. He then selected Rudolph Wurlitzer - whose screenplays included Two Lane Blacktop and Pat Garrett and Billy the Kid-to write the final Dune adaptation. But eight months and three drafts later, opinions were sharply divided as to the merits of the Scott/Wurlitzer collaboration. Again, great liberties had been taken with the novel, with Wurlitzer emphasizing its political facets and transforming the Fremen - the secretive, nomadic denizens of Arrakis - into the downtrodden galactic equivalent of today's urban poor. More contentious yet was an incestuous union between Paul and Jessica that rendered Alia both sister and daughter to Paul -a deviation from the book that infuriated Frank Herbert. But the final undoing of Ridley Scott's Dune was its projected $50 million price tag -a figure which De Laurentiis' major backer, Universal Pictures, considered far too high - an obvious irony, in that the eventual David Lynch production would cost Universal all of that, and more. Consequently, by September 1980, Scott had terminated his involvement with Dune and moved on to Blade Runner. By default, Dune seemed destined never to be made. But Dino De Laurentiis refused to pass on the project, and in 1981 - nine years after Arthur Jacobs had taken out his initial option on the novel - De Laurentiis renegotiated Herbert's contract, securing not only the rights to Dune, but to all of its sequels, both written and unwritten, as well. The fourth, and ultimately successful, attempt to turn Dune into a cinematic reality began in June 1981, with Raffaella De Laurentiis being assigned to the project by her father. Already a seasoned producer with several films to her credit, the distaff De Laurentiis began by making a number of inquiries into the availability of certain directors; but after attending a screening of The Elephant Man - directed by David Lynch - she knew for certain that she had found her man. \"I was very moved by The Elephant Man. But more importantly, I knew that I'd found the right director for Dune. The Elephant Man proved that David could handle both character and special effects, and that was the kind of mixture I wanted to include in Dune.\" Dino De Laurentiis contacted Lynch by phone, immediately intriguing the director with a thumbnail description of the plot. After reading the book for himself, Lynch was hooked. \"When I finished the novel, I was just knocked out. I'd seen Star Wars, of course; but to be honest, I wasn't all that crazy about it. For me, there was just too much on the surface in that film, and nothing else. Dune was different; it had believable characterizations and depth. In many ways, Herbert had created an internaladventure, one with a lot of emotional and physical textures. And I love textures.\" Textures had already played a major role in Lynch's early cinematic efforts, most of which were decidedly avant-garde in nature. A smattering of film festival awards for a short subject he directed opened the door for his acceptance into the American Film Institute advanced studies program in 1970. Two years later, Lynch began shooting his first feature film - Eraserhead. Filmed in sooty black-and-white, the project took five long years to complete, but its eventual success on the midnight movie circuit - not unlike Jodorowsky's El Topo years earlier - led to Lynch's being offered the directorial reins on The Elephant Man. This wide-screen, black-and-white examination of the real-life 19th century John Merrick, afflicted with a bizarre, disfiguring disease, earned Lynch Academy Award nominations for both direction and screenplay. After The Elephant Man, Lynch turned down an offer to direct Return of the Jedi, opting instead to take up residence at Zoetrope Studios where he began work on his own project, Ronnie Rocket-a characteristically baroque blending of aliens and rock 'n' roll. By 1981, however, Zoetrope was in severe financial trouble, and at about the time the studio finally closed down, Lynch received his fortuitous invitation to try his hand at cracking the curse of Dune. Impressed by Herbert's novel and De Laurentiis' plans for the film, Lynch moved into an office on the Universal lot and, by late 1981, was immersed in his own attempt to transform Dune into a workable screenplay. At first, he enlisted the collaborative aid of Eric Bergren and Christopher DeVore, with whom he had written The Elephant Man script. \"I figured Dune was such a giant project that I couldn't do it alone. So Eric, Chris and I wrote a treatment and a 200-page script together, which probably would've ended up as a four-hour movie. But then I realized that Chris and Eric were going off in a different direction from the one I'd intended, so we parted company. Then I asked Dino if I could do the script alone. He was a little skeptical at first; but later, when I showed him seventeen pages I'd done myself, he"],["27","The Shape of ‘Dune’","said it was the best work he'd seen so far, and I got the assignment.\" Before De Laurentiis gave the go-ahead to Lynch's solo effort on the Dune screenplay, the director spent a week picking Herbert's brain at the author's home - a six-and-one-half acre Ecological Demonstration Project situated on Washington's Olympia Peninsula, complete with wind generators, solar power and other instrumentalities designed to demonstrate that a high quality of life can be maintained with a minimal drain on the total energy system. From there, Lynch launched into the script, producing seven full drafts which - unlike previous attempts - strove diligently to maintain the tone and essence of the book. Herbert himself was much pleased with the adaptation, and in the months to come would be a frequent visitor to the Dune sets and one of the film's most vocal supporters. One of Lynch's first priorities was to hire a production designer who would be responsible for molding the shape of Dune. To this end, Anthony Masters joined the project shortly after Lynch first set up shop on the Universal lot. With over thirty years in production design, Masters had worked on more than fifty films, including such diverse projects as Lawrence of Arabia and 2001: A Space Odyssey. \"For Dune, David's first dictum was: 'If it's been done before, throw it outl' And although • some out And alt we were aware of, and had available, some of the design work done for the Jodorowsky and Scott versions of Dune, we immediately decided to throw that out too and start totally fresh. Lynch wanted his Dune to be more believable than the usual sci-fi projects because - even though the film is set in the year 10,991 - David very specifically didn't want the film to have a futuristic look. The people scattered throughout Dune's galaxy originated from earth, and if you look a few thousand years back in our own history you'll see that the Greeks were designing buildings very much like some of the banks we use today. So our Dune, from the start, was to be grounded in reality, to have the look of a period film even though it's set in the future. We even included some 1950-ish types of over-elaborate, functionless decoration. On the other hand, this film has some incredible elements that could only have sprung from David Lynch's head. We worked very closely together on this project, and I was constantly being surprised at what David was coming up with. He was completely approachable at all times-even when you'd find yourself thinking that his ideas were crazy and would never work. Then you'd build those ideas and try them out and find they weren't so crazy after all.\" Like Jodorowsky before them, Lynch and Masters immediately realized that their first design consideration lay in giving each of the four planets featured in Dune a distinct and recognizable environment. To achieve this, Masters broke down the primary elements into wood, gold, steel and stone. \"David and I first began by talking about the look of Caladan. We decided that this Atreides homeworld would be a lot like earth, filled with seas, huge forests and trees - which you never see, actually. But the interiors of Caladan were done with a rich wood finishing to CINEFEX 21 • 27"],["28","The Shape of ‘Dune’","28 • CINEFEX 21"],["29","The Shape of ‘Dune’","reflect Caladan's biosphere. For the Emperor's planet, Kaitain, we assumed that his enormous wealth would be reflected in buildings made of jade and solid gold. So for the interior of his throne room, we based our designs on that famous Moorish fortress in Spain, the Alhambra. Giedi Prime, the Harkonnen's homeworld, was where David really let go. He's really in love with machinery; and in fact he once said that, given the choice, he'd rather visit an abandoned factory than Disneyland. So Giedi Prime is very over-industrialized, a planet covered with seas of oil and containing buildings that are about as attractive as a fireplug. Everything there is either black metal or green porcelain, with no enrichment or any design idea to please the eye. It's all just practical, lumpy equipment. And on Arrakis - Dune itself - life is very harsh and primitive. The Fremen have built everything out of solid rock, using laser cutters to create the vast, rectangular, clean-cut shapes of their hiding places, the sietches. We also incorporated some very recognizable stylistic architecture in the film. You can see obvious Victorian influences on Giedi Prime, for example, and Mayan motifs as well as Egyptian pyramids on Arrakis. In fact, all of the mosaics and tiling seen in the Arrakeen palace on Arrakis are a direct result of a trip David took to Venice. He visited the cathedral in St. Mark's Square there and was really impressed, coming back to me and saying: 'Heyl Let's get that mosaic look into Dune/' So that's where that Byzantine influence on Arrakis comes from.\" One of the earliest - and most difficult - design problems facing Masters revolved around the ornithopters, Herbert's airborne vehicles that traverse the skies of Arrakis using supple winglike appendages. \"But we quickly decided that there was no possible way we could design a craft with flapping wings that wouldn't be laughed off the screen. That decision was the easy part: actually doing the design was quite a chore.\" After lengthy discussions with Lynch, Masters began sketching, submitting, analyzing and resubmitting various approaches -going over more than 150 ornithopter designs before the film's two basic crafts were decided upon. The Atreides 'thopter evolved into a diamond-shaped vehicle capable of unfolding into a boxlike structure for flight, then reverting to its original shape upon landing to protect it from the harsh sandstorms found on Dune. The Harkonnen ornithopters are made of three interconnecting steel tubes, giving the craft a dark, heavy, sinister look. But what of the nonexistent wings? \"David came up with a concept he called 'ether benders' - large magnetic pads fixed on the bottom of the 'thopters which used anti-gravitational fields to push them away from the surface of Arrakis. This concept made the use of wings totally superfluous. In fact, over the two years we worked on the Dune designs, we started at point A, worked our way through the entire design alphabet and then, circularly, arrived at A again. The spice harvesters are another good example of that. At the very beginning, much of Dune- its props and machinery and weapons - had an organic and then bizarre, insect-like appearance. After countless design changes, the harvester finally came out looking like a beetle again.\" Veteran production designer Tony Masters was among the first to join Lynch's team, working closely with the director to conceptualize and then create the four divergent worlds of Dune. / The miniatures crew adds final detailing to the elegant palace of Padishah Emperor Shaddam IV - incorporated later into an even larger complex designed to represent the imperial capital on Kaitain. / A stagehand makes adjustments on a Guild ship as it hovers over the Emperor's landing field. The fiberglass model - simplified to its relatively featureless form at Lynch's insistence - was mounted on a small camera crane and then lowered into one of the many slots cut into the miniature set. / Castle Caladan - constructed by miniatures expert Emilio Ruiz del Rio and his crew. / Stagehands lower a bank of outboard motors into a pool of water beneath the castle to create wave effects. A blue screen in the rear allowed for the later incorporation of storm clouds. CINEFEX 21 • 29"],["30","The Shape of ‘Dune’","first sculpted the third-stage Guild navigator in plasticine. then cast in foam rubber and Luigi Rocchetti apply a few last-minute brush strokes on the set. / Padishah Emperor confronts the visiting thirdretinue of first- and secondstagers. Floating in a cloud actually orange smoke - the represents the pinnacle of melange-induced mutation. / ultimately rejected because funds. / On stage, camera operator Ken Worringham, supervisor Barry Nolan use a foreground element. Nolan and his Van der Veer Photo Effects joined the production midway through principal miniature work, animation and postproduction opticals. Carlo Rambaldi and crew The imposing figure was assembled over an aluminum and steel armature. Cable-actuated control mechanisms provided facial and body movements. / Rambaldi (rear) and makeup artist Shaddam IV (Jose Ferrer) stage navigator and his of orange spice gas - third-stage navigator A second-stage navigator makeup, created by Christopher Tucker, was of insufficient time and David Lynch and effects lightbox to line up a background clip with a photography, providing 30 • CINEFEX 21 Although a production designer usually contributes to a film's storyboards - \"Originally David didn't want to use storyboards at all,\" offered Raffaella De Laurentiis, \"I really had to push him on that\" - Masters soon realized that the enormous workload generated by Dune would obstruct his personal involvement in this area. Therefore, production illustrators Mentor Huebner and George Jenson were hired to storyboard Dune's special effects. Apogee board artist Marty Kline also contributed to the film during his company's involvement with Dune. But perhaps the most influential of Masters' artistic aides was production illustrator Ron Miller. An artist and writer whose work has appeared in dozens of magazines and books, Miller is a past director of the Smithsonian Institution's exhibits of futuristic and astronomical art. In fact, it was one of Miller's own collections of astronomical art - an excursion through the solar system titled The Grand Tour- that landed Miller his job on Dune in the first place. \"Apparently David thought my paintings of Mars looked just like Dune: and the next thing I knew, I was being flown out from my studio in Virginia to the Dune production offices in Los Angeles.\" Miller began his year-long stint on Dune by producing fifteen large-format conceptual paintings, along with a couple dozen smaller illustrations, based on some line and magic marker drawings provided by Tony Masters - the sum total of Dune's preproduction art at that point. \"But I quickly moved into other areas, too. Although no one really asked me, I soon found myself evolving into Dune's unofficial science advisor, trying to keep things as empirically reasonable as possible. At one point, I even brought in my own astronomical consultant, William Hartman, to talk to David about the more scientific aspects of the production. Of course, the novel was the ultimate reference for questions about anything. Everybody had copies of it - and if you didn't, they gave you one. Frank Herbert even provided the filmmakers with a pronunciation list of his rather strange vocabulary.\" Not long after Miller began working on the film, his wife - artist Judith Weiss - also became involved, producing a number of folded paper models of sets and machines which served to help Lynch visualize the look of the art department drawings. \"As I worked on Dune,\" Miller continued, \"I found out that Lynch's three favorite things in the world were small triangles, black rubber and little twisty wires. I also found that David hated over-detailed models - the kind that look as if they've been dipped in glue and rolled in model parts. So Dune's unusual, clean-lined ship designs all go back simply to things David cares for.\" Perhaps the most eagerly-awaited visual element on which Miller worked were the film's massive, mile-long sandworms. \"Boy, did we go round and round on those! David had come across a photograph of a piebald elephant trunk - a sort of mottled red and black - that he liked very much. So that was the coloration he decided on for the worms. That decision was made fairly early on and adhered to throughout the produc-"],["31","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 31 SS"],["32","The Shape of ‘Dune’","32 • CINEFEX 21"],["33","The Shape of ‘Dune’","tion, even though when you see the worms in the film, they've been so covered by dust and sand that they look more gray than anything else, But the overall shape of the worms, particularly the mouths, wasn't as easy to nail down.\" Even though Lynch and Masters were unfamiliar with the work of John Schoenherr - for many years considered the definitive Dune artist - Masters' earliest drawings for the worms bore a striking resemblance to Schoenherr's, particularly in their use of a circular, three-lobed mouth. \"Then the worms went through a lot of changes,\" Miller explained, \"mostly having to do with varying their degree of obsceneness. After all, we were all more than well aware of the fact that we were dealing with the greatest phallic symbols in the universe, and that we would have to be very careful about our worm designs and the camera angles we would use to shoot them. Eventually, though, the worms came around full circle, right back to the drawings Tony had done that looked like Schoenherr's. And I was happy. I liked that three-lobed mouth, and argued for it for a long time.\" After long, devoted months of creative effort, the Dune project had not yet received an official go-ahead. Preproduction was finally authorized, however, in June 1982, and with all systems in full swing, thoughts turned to locations. North Africa, England, Tunisia, India, Australia and Italy were all considered as possible settings; but Mexico City was finally decided upon as the production's home base. This choice seemed ideal for a number of practical reasons. Due to the country's depressed economy, the De Laurentiis' knew they would get twice as much for their money in Mexico as they could anywhere else. Further, Mexico City was at least somewhat close to the Samalayuca desert, a locale Lynch deemed aesthetically perfect for the four-week Arrakis location shoot. Finally, Mexico City was chosen because of the well-established production facilities of Estudios Churubusco, a filmmaking complex with eight full soundstages and three backlots. In September 1982, Lynch and his company moved south and began gathering the remainder of those people, both cast and crew, who would finally turn Herbert's book into motion picture reality. The majority of the Dune unit, of course, would be Mexican. But other members of the team would reflect a truly international scope - Italian, English, French, Spanish, Venezuelan and American. As befitting Dune's epic scale, between 600 and 900 crew and cast members were variously involved in the production, a figure which fluctuated as project requirements demanded. Life in the world's largest city - with a population of 17 million - was not without its problems, however. The 7200-foot altitude of the metropolis, plus an ongoing round of intestinal sickness, frequent power outages and the uncustomarily slow Mexican lifestyle proved constantly annoying to many involved in the production. A more serious problem centered on Mexican customs practices which, in effect, held much of the film's incoming equipment and supplies in quarantine until necessary payments were made to release them. But despite such aggravations, David Lynch found the overall experience exhilarating, \"We had a real happy family on Dune, no doubt about it. On some films, there's a lot of factionalism and people pulling apart, but on this movie everybody pulled together. In fact, the only thing the cast and crew had a problem with was Mexico. Not a day went past without some complaint about the food or illnesses or some weird foul-up. Personally, though, I found Mexico very exciting. I'd be out being driven to the studio or whatever, and I'd just lean back and let the landscape wash over me. The architecture, the culture - it really got to me and started some boss ideas kicking around in my head.\" And so, on March 30, 1983, more than twenty years after its literary debut, Dune went before the cameras at Churubusco. Though color was mandated for the film, Lynch-a strong advocate of black-and-white photography - urged his veteran cinematographer, Freddie Francis, to give Dunea moody, almost monochromatic look. For optimum effect, Francis - who had previously photographed The Elephant Man and was thus already well-attuned to his director's aesthetic sensitivitiesdecided to employ the Lightflex system he had used with considerable success on The French Lieutenant's Woman. \"The Lightflex is a mechanism which allows a controlled prefogging of film. The unit is attached to the front of the camera. There is a curved plastic glass front that you shoot through; and above, there is a frosted screen with a low-wattage electric light shining through it. Light comes through the screen, hits the window in front of the lens, and the lens then shoots through this window, which is illuminated. It's all done in-camera. If one is doing low-key work -which is David's trademark - then the Lightflex gives a beautiful texture, which I suppose could be likened to candlelight. Specifically for Dune, I used the Lightflex for any number of sequences. For instance, there is a moment in the ornithopter where Paul sniffs at some raw melange, and everything whites out. That was done with the Lightflex.\" Use of the Lightflex system - developed by British cinematographer Gerry Turpin- entailed fitting each of the Dune cameras with a small generator, a filter hood and a rheostat to vary the light intensities. By dialing in the reflected light, Francis was able to gain a far greater latitude over contrast control, fill light and color tints. \"On Giedi Prime, I enhanced the green in the set by putting a bit of green light in my Lightflex. You can also bring up background details, and use very little light on a set. All you do is set the Lightflex to a certain density to fill in your shadows. So much of Dune was shot on low lighting levels - in fact, Dino was always pushing for more light, and David for less - that the control offered by the Lightflex really was a comfort.\" Another major participant in the on-stage work was mechanical effects supervisor Kit West. An Oscar-winner for Raiders of the Lost Ark, West had just completed Return of the Jedi when the call came to join the Dune unit. One of his first assignments The personal body shield - a stylized suit of armor composed of impenetrable slabs of solid electricity - was first demonstrated in the knife fight between Paul Atreides and warmaster Gurney Halleck. Working over live-action plate material shot at Churubusco, animators at Van der Veer first rendered the faceted shield formations using line drawings and basic rotoscope techniques. Each individual image was then broken down into specific surfaces, with a separate animation cel for each shield segment. A computercontrolled ripple wheel system built into an optical printer provided the desired diffusion and threedimensional effect. / Jeffrey Burks reviews pencil tests for a body shield animation shot. Burks and Richard Malzahn - head of Van der Veer's rotoscope and animation department - devoted nine months to animating the sequence. CINEFEX 21 • 33"],["34","The Shape of ‘Dune’","lighting for a closeup insert of the third-stage navigator. / Baron Vladimir Harkonnen (Kenneth McMillan) relaxes Giannetto De Rossi makeup involving numerous latex character requires anti-grav designer Bob Ringwood, the canvas and rubber suit was impart an authentic sense of fleshiness. / Although the majority of the flying scenes photographed on the Giedi Prime sets, the finale - in which the Baron's anti-grav ejecting him through a hole in the palace wall and into photographed against a blue lends a steadying hand as Effects cinematographer James Devis checks the as his facial boils are lanced - a convincing appliances. / McMillan - whose grossly obese suspensor units for support - models his fat suit for director Lynch. Conceived by costume filled with silicone gel to were rigged and system goes haywire, the gaping maw of a sandworm - required an elaborate tumbling mechanism and was screen. / Flying unit supervisor John Stirber his aerial wire rig lifts McMillan off the floor. 34 • CINEFEX 21 was to produce the robot fighter Paul Atreides (hyle Maclachlan) encounters during his training. Lowered from the ceiling of the training room set, the fighter robot was conceived as a deadly, brass-sheathed mechanism equipped with hydraulic rams and spears sufficient to test the mettle of any warrior. \"It seemed to us,\" said Tony Masters, \"that Kyle had to fight this thing in a controlled area, since the robot itself was going to be very limited in its movements. The robot fighter basically evolved out of a simple cylinder covered with a series of disks, each of which concealed a weapon that would do something. In this particular instance, though, form followed function. We didn't know what weapons we could make and what actions they could do. So we got Kit West involved in that, and had him just invent things he felt the robot could do best. Then, after Kit told us what he could do, we designed those things into the robot.\" Once the design was approved, West and his crew manufactured the fighting machine in their Churubusco workshop. \"The actual cylinder itself, and much of the detailing, was made out of fiberglass,\" West explained. \"Then we realized that there were going to be about six layers on that robot that actually did something, so we sat down again and figured out which layer would do what and how it would be controlled. The systems we came up with eventually involved pneumatics, radio control, cabling, plus compressed air lines and vacuums since the objects on that robot had to fly out and retract. We also ran a gas line into the tip that Paul melts with his weirding module: this was simply wax that melted after the gas was set off by a small igniter near the end of the line. Incidentally, my crew and I decided that since the camera would be very close to the action, we couldn't get away with putting rubber tips on the spears and things. So all those dangerous knives and spears there had real brass finishes on them. \"But physically controlling that robot was probably the trickjest thing we had to do. I designed it so that it would not only advance or retreat on an opponent, but revolve on its own axis as well. In the ceiling above the robot was a large steel turntable about five feet in diameter, on which was a pivoting horizontal elbow made of metal that broke in the middle. The robot hung off one of the ends of this elbow on a straight shaft equipped with pulley blocks. In other words, the turntable rotated in a circle while the elbow straightened out or contracted as the robot revolved around this shaft. The whole apparatus was controlled on levers activated by pneumatic rams. It took six people to operate the robot when it was down and fighting, plus another six on the mechanism which raised and lowered and revolved the whole thing. For some of the close shots of the fighter throwing out its spears, we took an appropriate sectionalized piece of the robot, hung it on a lamp stand and operated it that way. Also on the robot, we had a little lens at the top of the cylinder that was always supposed to be facing the actor, since this was how the fighter tracked its opponent. That lens was radio-controlled by John Hatt.\" In addition to the functional robot, a number of balsa wood dummies were also constructed"],["35","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 35"],["36","The Shape of ‘Dune’","36 4 CINFFEX 21"],["37","The Shape of ‘Dune’","for shots where various parts of it had to be blown off - both in Paul's fight, and then later in the Fremen training sequence. Extensive wire work was also featured in Dune, ranging from the relative simplicity of gently floating glow globes to the kinetic attack of a small, but deadly 'hunter-seeker' unit. \"The 'hunter-seeker' is supposed to be an anti-grav assassination device attracted to the slightest motion,\" West explained. \"If it hits you, the tip comes off and races through your body, tearing everything away in its path until it comes to the nearest large organ - not a nice way to go. That prop was basically turned out of brass, with a nose cone spun in plexiglass that we did on a lathe. An actual hypodermic needle was on the end of it. There's one shot from the hunter-seeker's point of view as it's stalking Paul. That we did by sticking an eighteen-inch diameter vacuformed plexiglass half-globe in front of the camera, with a lot of little mechanisms inside that moved and swung around to suggest sensor devices and a gyro.\" Most of the shots, though, were simply marionetted, with the prop hanging on thin wires. \"Eventually, someone opens the door and Paul - who's been standing stock-still - has to grab this thing as it flashes past him. We shot the prop down a wire by compressed air; and when Paul grabbed it, we just cut the wire so that he could turn around and smash it against the wall. Kyle timed it just perfectly.\" To complete the effect, small squibs were simultaneously set off in both the prop and in the wall section where MacLachlan crushed The most impressive showcase for the wire crew's talents was with the villainous Baron Vladimir Harkonnen (Kenneth McMillan) who - as in Herbert's book - is so grossly corpulent that he cannot walk. Instead, his weight is carried by anti-grav suspensor mechanisms. The Baron's costume proved to be quite a challenge for British costume designer Bob Ringwood, whose previous credits include the wardrobe creation for John Boorman's Excalibur. 'Vladimir is supposed to weigh about four hundred pounds,\" said Ringwood, \"and Ken McMillan, although he is a short and round man, only weighs about two hundred. So I designed and fabricated a hundred-pound fat suit for him in Los Angeles that was made primarily out of thin canvas and a strong rubber -I used colostomy bags, actually, which are already flesh-colored. Then I divided the suit up into pockets, which were filled with a silicone gel that gave a nice fleshy movement to the suit. I also used colostomy bag rubber for the bits of flesh that bulged out from between the folds in the Baron's suit.\" Ringwood ultimately fabricated two fat suits - one of which was laced with tiny tubes through which cold water could be pumped to cool off the actor. Both were equipped with a cup and catheter system developed by NASA that allowed for safe urination while in costume. By the time McMillan had spent about an hour-and-a-half suiting up, the actor had acquired almost enough extra pounds to place him in the same heavyweight category as Herbert's original character. Giannetto De Rossi's makeup department completed the Giedi Prime - the grimy, over-industrialized home of the Harkonnens - was featured in only a half-dozen shots, but proved to be one of the most complex effects challenges in the film. / Prominently showcased was the Face Burner - an oilburning melange processor designed to resemble the Baron. The large model was first sculpted in clay, then covered with a plaster waste mold and cast in fiberglass. When finished, it was photographed in front of a blue screen. / Richard Malzahn storyboards the Giedi Prime sequence. / Barry Nolan prepares to shoot a background plate of the Giedi Prime model - a modular structure assembled from sculpted panels produced by Gus Ramsden. The plate will later be composited with the bluescreen Face Burner footage, plus a small cable car model and various light images. / Camera and model rigger Lynn Ledgerwood checks the alignment on another Giedi Prime establishing shot, produced under the auspices of veteran matte artist Albert Whitlock. The painting - rendered by Syd Dutton - would later be enhanced with an array of live-action and miniature elements. CINEFEX 21 • 37"],["38","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Model unit supervisor Brian Smithies dusts off the planet Caladan prior to shooting. The three-dimensional model Atreides armada entering the Arrakis. / Barry Nolan makes later refinished by Smithies and his crew. / David Lynch examines a heighliner setup modified by Nolan and his motion control programmer, problems with the Mexican electrical supply - including brownouts, blackouts and employment of a dedicated transformers to provide the sensitive, computerized unit was one element among many in the complex composite shot of the Guild heighliner for its space-folding journey to a lighting check on the cylindrical heighliner model - constructed originally by Apogee, but through Van der Veer's effects camera, trackmounted on a motion control rig designed and Eric Swenson. Recurring voltage fluctuations, voltage spikes - necessitated the generator and a series of with a stable and reliable source of power. 38 • CINEFEX 21 picture. \"The Baron's facial makeup-all those pimples and boils - took about three hours a day to apply,\" said De Rossi. \"There were thirty-five separate latex appliances that we had to put onto his face, plus a latex section over his hair - which we also shaved in a terrible way.\" De Rossi and his crew also provided McMillan with a fake chest piece, complete with hair. \"It was very difficult to find the proper way for the Baron to look. We tried three different approaches before we had a makeup that he could wear and also move and act in. Our second makeup, in fact, was just too heavy for the Baron to wear and still be able to hang up on wires for his flying scenes.\" For his flying unit chief, Kit West selected John Stirber, an American wire expert he had utilized before. \"Ken was just marvelous about the wire work,\" said West. \"He really enjoyed it. Before he ever started flying down in Mexico, though, we'd had him up on wires in Los Angeles many times while he was being fitted for his fat suit. By the time he got to Churubusco, he was quite used to the flying rig. Under his costume, Ken had on a pair of cut-off Levis that were a size too big for him that we'd lined with lamb's wool to keep him comfortable, because he was on those wires continuously one day for six hours, which I think is some kind of record. A wire was then attached to each hip at a pivot point, and the wires ran to overhead rails on top of the set. John used various gauges of piano wire, and we hid them through a variety of means - lighting, painting them, dulling them down. David had an odd attitude about the wires -he simply didn't worry about them. The only thing he cared about was that they not show, which made us sweat a bit.\" Though Lynch was apparently unconcerned about the wires, others on the staff were. Tony Masters, for one, helped immeasurably in the misdirection process by incorporating vertical lines and dressings into his sets. Freddie Francis did likewise with the lighting, devising a means of balancing foreground and background illumination until the wires eventually blended in with the prevailing set colors. The sets themselves were often problematic, though, in that some of them - most especially a narrow staircase - were so restricted spacewise that the five or six operators required to fly McMillan had to work remotely off closed circuit video monitors. Some of the wire rigging was quite elaborate. \"After the Harkonnens take over Dune \" said Kit West. \"David had a scene where the Baron was happily flying round and round in a steam bath. So we built quite an interesting rig to fly him for that shot. Overhead, in the rafters, was a thick metal shaft off of which ran a horizontal steel arm about twelve feet long. At the end of this arm was a small wire cage with a rotating seat on which our wire man sat. In front of him, the operator had a bicycle wheel that he could turn around like the wheel on an automobile - and this wheel was connected to the wires that flew the Baron, whose wire rig hung straight down from the operator's cage. So not only could we revolve the operator on this arm as if he were on a centrifuge, but the operator could at the same time turn the Baron around so that he was facing the camera when he was supposed to be.\" An even more complex rig was required for the Baron's demise. At the height of the Fremen assault on Arrakeen, Alia takes advantage of the diversion, slashing the Baron with a poisonous gom jabbar and then pulling out the controlling plugs on his anti-grav suspensor belt, sending the evil Harkonnen ruler into an uncontrolled spin before he is sucked out through a hole in the wall and straight into a worm's mouth. \"We shot Ken against a blue screen at Churubusco, using a double-pivoted wire rig,\" continued West. \"He could rotate and, through a ball-swivel joint, suddenly change the direction of his spin at the same time. McMillan was quite game about all this, but I know it couldn't have been pleasant. He was looking pretty green near the end. We also had a camera on a special rig spinning in the opposite direction Ken was rotating in, and then we did a long pull-back at the same time. So it looked as if Ken was moving far off into the distance.\" To augment the Baron's rapidly diminishing form, an optically induced reverse zoom was also added to the shot by Van der Veer. Not all of McMillan's flying scenes required wire work. For closeups, West attached the actor to the end of a counterweighted teeter-totter board mounted on a small trolley. As McMillan's weight was effectively 'zeroed out' by the counterweight, an acceptable floating effect was achieved. \"Between shots, Ken couldn't just sit down, so we made a rig that looked somewhat like the frame of a child's swing set and hooked him up to it in a standing position. That rig took most of his weight off the floor and allowed him to rest.\" Another major effects-oriented undertaking that had to be perfected for principal photography was the design and construction of the Guild navigator which visits the court of Padishah Emperor Shaddam IV early in the film. Represented in the scene are three different stages of an evolutionary process Fought about by the c brought about by the copious consumption of melange. In their most advanced third-stage form, the Guild navigators are empowered with the ability to 'fold space' - thus enabling their heighliner spacecraft to transit between planets without actually moving at all. First-stage navigators, charged with the responsibility of caring for their more advanced brethren, are still recognizably human. \"David wanted the first-stage navigators to suggest that they were beginning to change,\" said Giannetto De Rossi. \"So we did many simple things to the actors portraying the firststagers, like shaving their heads and gluing on small quarterinch rubber tubes that ran off of simple foam appliances, suggesting that their heads had cracked open and were oozing spice which was a tinted gelatin. David Lynch also wanted a lot of the extras playing members of the Emperor's court to be soldiers who'd lost pieces of themselves - noses, chins, ears -in battles. So, again, I added some simple plastic and foam appliances to the extras to suggest that they had had metal prosthetics put on their faces to replace the parts they had lost."],["39","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINFFEX 21 • 39"],["40","The Shape of ‘Dune’","40 4 CINEFEX 21"],["41","The Shape of ‘Dune’","David likes weird biology. He even hired a very tall Mexican with acromegaly to play a soldier.\" Second-stage navigators - humans more advanced along the spice mutational trail - were represented by actors wearing strange leather hoods, ostensibly to conceal their features, but in actuality because their makeups were never completed. \"The second-stage makeup was very elaborate,\" said De Rossi, \"and was done by Christopher Tucker, who had done John Hurt's makeup for The Elephant Man. The mouth was like a long, narrow trumpet, which made the man's face look like a white elephant with skin problems. But since the second-stagers were only intended to be featured in a couple of short scenes anyway, it was finally decided that there was no time or money to include them in the film. So these makeups were dropped.\" Creation of the third-stage navigator - described in the script as a 'fleshy grasshopper' - was clearly not a job for the makeup department, however, and plans for its development began with Tony Masters. \"Actually, the design of the third-stager started one day while David and I were in a restaurant and David began doodling these designs on paper napkins-just an outline, really, in the Picasso style, with a couple of dots for eyes. David is forever doodling on paper napkins. So I took one of his rather strange drawings, combined it with some ideas I knew he had in his head, and drew my own version of the third-stage navigator, which David accepted completely. Then Carlo Rambaldi took my drawing and gave it his own input, making the navigator a bit more slug-like.\" Carlo Rambaldi, a long-time De Laurentiis collaborator -and a multiple Oscar-winner for King Kong, Alien and E.T. -joined the Dune company in mid-1982. The task of transforming the navigator design into a three-dimensional figure began in the master creature creator's Northridge studio, with Laurie Marems, a sculptor and general assistant to Rambaldi for the past six years, and an expert crew of carvers- Willy Whitten, Tom Connors, Edmund Shumpert and Pat Newman. \"We were all working on Alia and the worms and the navigator simultaneously,\" recalled Marems, \"but the large third-stager was literally the biggest project. He had to be put up on top of a big, heavy metal structure in Carlo's shop because he was sculpted out of $13,000 worth of plasticine that weighed about two-and-a-half tons. Internally, that sculpture also had a steel support system to hold it all together. We even had chains hanging from the ceiling and attached to the top of the sculpture to support the supports!\" When finished, the navigator was more than sixteen feet long, with a head height of six feet. Working under Rambaldi's direction, Ralph Cobos and a small crew then applied plaster to the sculpture in different areas, producing a total of thirty sectional molds - molds from which latex pieces would later be pulled and reassembled to jigsaw the ponderous navigator back together again. But for all their painstaking work, the third-stage navigator was due for some hard knocks. \"We shipped the big navigator down to Churubusco by truck,\" Rambaldi recalled. \"But the trucks were Suspended in orbit over Caladan, the massive Guild heighliner awaits the arrival of the Atreides ships. / Brian Smithies, Gus Ramsden and effects technician Rodney Fuller prepare for a shot of the Atreides fleet entering the heighliner. Multiple groupings, suspended from wires, were photographed as motion control bluescreen elements for later compositing with additional ship formations, all heading for the heighliner doorway. / A thirty-inch Atreides ship was positioned against black velvet for a still photo session. Prints were then made in various sizes, laid out on glass, and photographed to represent multiple ship formations viewed in the distance. / Foreground miniatures maestro Emilio Ruiz del Rio examines the wing section on the largest of the Atreides ships. / Danielle Verse and Christine Overs add intricate ornamentation to a blowup section of the heighliner doorway. CINEFEX 21 • 41"],["42","The Shape of ‘Dune’","enlarged sections built - was used for the smallerscale background plate over which the Atreides ships are immense craft. / Modelmaker Gus Ramsden makes final adjustments to an Atreides wing section incorporated docking bay set. During the scene, as the camera moves protruding ship in the center designated docking port. / subsequent shot featuring the third-stage navigator's sealed box as it rises to the Worringham photographs Another Guild heighliner doorway - one of two shown approaching the within his heighliner up, a pneumatic cylinder concealed behind the pulls the craft into its Ramsden redressed the fiberglass structure for a heighliner cockpit. Ken the slow ascent - which was ultimately cut from the film. 42 - 4 CINEFEX 21 changed in El Paso, and the navigator wasn't properly repacked. He got to the studio with one whole side of his head crushed in. Luckily, I already had a little studio set up at Churubusco, so I could repair the navigator, but I still had to redo his whole In the film, the monstrous creature was transported in a forty-foot-long, glass-faced chamber filled with orange spice gas - actually tinted smoke. The chamber, open from the back, allowed the navigator - mounted on a steel ram - to be pushed forward as though it were swimming out of the gas. \"We built only one big navigator,\" said Rambaldi, \"and, depending on the shot, it took between five and twenty-six people to operate the cable controls. Although it wasn't as complicated as some of the worms I made, the big third-stage model had forty-five points of movement, including six in each arm, six in the mouth and two in the eyes - which could dilate by small, controllable rubber diaphragms. We also had a tube in the mouth to blow out spice gas, to make it look as if it were breathing this stuff in and out. The skin was made of foam latex, and his inner body was a polyurethane core, with an aluminum and steel support Carlo Rambaldi and crew were also responsible for creating the fetus of Alia, Paul Atreides' younger sister. \"We used the fetus for three shots in the film,\" Rambaldi recalled. \"Once for Paul's vision when it is being born, once during Jessica's Water of Life ceremony where you see it floating in blackness and it stares up at her, and a third shot that was cut from the film.\" Based on the physiogomy of Alicia Roanne Witt -the young actress playing Alia - the fetus began as a clay sculpture by Laurie Marems, with Rambaldi himself completing the job. Approximately ten inches long, the tiny figure was constructed from latex rubber and provided with nine points of movement, including the ability to breath via small internal bladders connected to air hoses blown into by off-camera technicians. The fetus could also opens its mouth and move its arms, neck and legs; and since so much movement was required in such a small object, Rambaldi dispensed with an internal skeleton. \"David was always very interested in that fetus,\" recalled Bruno Rubeo, one of Rambaldi's cable operators. \"In fact, when he first visited Carlo's studio, Lynch stared at the clay sculpture of the fetus for a second and then reached out and pulled the back of its head all out of shape. He liked it better that way.\" For the shot of the fetus being born, a three-foot-wide fiberglass uterus, tapering down to a foot, was contructed by Kit West. Filled with tinted glycerine, the uterus was also detailed with small fibers and streaks of color to suggest veins. The fetus was then placed in the glycerine and shot at 48 frames per second while being lit from outside the translucent fiberglass construction. To further enhance the shot, Lynch asked that the uterus and fetus be filmed through a glass-bottomed tray of water, a technique which effectively broke up the image and light into slow wave patterns. Of utmost concern were the legendary sandworms of Arrakis. With full realization that if they were not altogether convincing, much of the credibility of the film would be lost, Rambaldi gave the worm development project top priority. Modifying the basic Masters/Lynch design, Rambaldi added to the original threelobed mouth a second, inner three-lipped mouth lined with thousands of triangular teeth. This double-valve mouth was not only deduced to be scientifically sound -as a protection for the worms against accidentally ingesting the sand through which they traveled - but was considered visually dramatic as well. In the course of developing a workable worm design, considerable study was made into the appearance and behavior of real worms - sometimes with comical results. \"I remember how, one day, Carlo and an assistant dumped a carton full of earthworms on David's desk while we were still working in L.A.,\" Ron Miller recalled. \"Here's Tony and Carlo standing around while David's hunched over a worm, staring at it through a magnifying glass and saying, 'Where's its mouth?' David couldn't find it. Later, he looked up, smiled and said: 'I think this one is tired. Let's get another.\" In all, fifteen complete worms of various sizes were constructed. The two largest were twenty-two feet long, with fully articulated heads equipped with three-beaked mouths that opened and closed, and mechanized inner mouths. These threefoot diameter worms were used throughout the film for shots requiring detailed foreground action, such as when Paul and Jessica are attacked while they are hiding in a rock formation in the deep desert. Three other worms, fifteen feet long and two feet in diameter, were also built. Both the large and mediumsize worms were controlled by inner mechanisms - designed by Rambaldi, with assistance from his principal machinist, Steve Townsend - that allowed them a variety of cable-controlled movements, including swinging from side to side and rearing straight up. Finally, ten three-foot-long 'sausage' worms were built for use in long shots and as background filler. These sausage worms were guided by simple tracks buried beneath the miniature desert and worm sets. Not equipped with inner articulating mechanisms, the sausage worms were capable of moving from side to side simply by following the direction in which the tracks had been laid. In addition to the complete worms, two worm sections were also constructed. \"We built one big section that was only the head and neck,\" said Rambaldi. \"It was twelve feet long and five feet in diameter, and was so big it could easily swallow a human head. In fact, that's mainly what we used this section for - swallowing. It had a heavily-detailed outer mouth and an inner mouth lined with teeth, and we used it for many closeups - like swallowing the harvester, the camera and the Sardaukar army at the end of the picture.i Yet another large worm piece was a twenty-foot-long tail section, three feet in diameter, which was skeletonized with a wooden frame, packed with polyurethane and covered in latex - the same general construction principle used to fabricate the head. \"All the different size worms were first sculpted in clay,\""],["43","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 43"],["44","The Shape of ‘Dune’","44 • CINEFEX 21"],["45","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Rambaldi explained, \"then packed with polyfoam and covered with latex rubber after being pulled from plaster molds. Each was hand-painted to David's instructions.\" A crew of twenty-four machinists, moldmakers and sculptors worked on the assignment. Rambaldi's unit also produced the worm riders -tiny representations of the Fremen warriors who have tamed these gigantic creatures. Over six hundred small worm riders were produced - some molded in rubber, with the tinier riders being made through a jewelry casting process. All together, there were three different-sized riders, with the majority being approximately one inch high. Glued onto the upper head and backs of the worms, and prominently featured during Dune's final battle scenes, some of the miniature worm riders were detailed - equipped with tiny 'maker hooks' and lines, for example - although most were simply humanoid in shape. Even as principal photography continued with relative smoothness, trouble was brewing on the visual effects front. Having spent nearly a year soliciting bids from most of Hollywood's premier effects facilities, the De Laurentiis organization ultimately engaged the services of John Dykstra and Apogee to produce most of the miniatures, bluescreen work and postproduction opticals required for Dune. However, midway through principal photography, and after months of Apogee involvement, Dykstra and De Laurentiis parted ways. \"Essentially, I wanted to do the work in Los Angeles and they wanted to do it in Mexico,\" Dykstra explained. \"I spent three months down there with a small crew, and after a careful study of the mechanical demands we felt were imposed by the shoot - such as the sophisticated motion control work best done in model environments versus such things as electric power in Mexico that could go from 90 to 140 volts to nothing in a minute -and after shooting a trial setup of the worms in the Apogee parking lot, it seemed that working in L.A. would be the best way to go. But the De Laurentiis' wanted to build and shoot the models down in Mexico. It was a classic case of: We can save so much money shooting in Mexico, it's the only way to go. The other side of the coin being: 'Yes, but the quality's going to suffer.' And that's basically where the parting of ways came in. I could not see any way clear of doing the work in a second-rate manner - especially for that movie, and especially for David Lynch, who was really busting his ass to make a great-looking film. I was very disappointed at the time; but it probably came out for the best, frankly, because had I acquiesced and done the stuff in Mexico, then I would have been quite unhappy with the product from my own point of view. But I certainly have the greatest respect for the people who came in after I left and completed the work - especially knowing the conditions under which they had to do it.\" Although Dykstra was unable to see the project through, evidences of Apogee involvement still remain in the film - most apparently in the form of the highly-detailed and articulated Atreides ornithopter constructed in their stateside model shop. Minor bluescreen elements and VistaVision background plates were also accomplished, but their most significant legacy was the 'Blue Max' system, developed specifically for the project and left behind with a group of technicians when the rest of the Apogee team departed. \"The Blue Max is essentially a front projection bluescreen system,\" said Dykstra. \"We'd had the opportunity to use bluescreen front projection on a small scale in the past: but with the size and prestige of this project, we realized that this would be a perfect opportunity to build our Blue Max system and then rent it to the Dune crew for their use down in Mexico. The main reason we wanted to go with front projection blue on Dune was that we were working with such enormous sets - a hundred feet by a hundred feet. It didn't seem practical to do it with traditional frontlit blue because the number of arc lights we would have needed to get enough illumination to carry depth of field over such large sets would have been incredible. Plus, the size of the screen itself had to be very large, which rendered it impractical to build a transmission blue screen. So what we did was to bring this front projection technique to a head.\" Apogee's resident engineers, Don Trumbull and Jon Erland, worked on the project, along with Stephen Fog and optics experts David Grafton and Paul Burk. A difference of opinion on the best way to proceed resulted in the concurrent development of two separate systems, each with a somewhat different optical design. As time grew short, however, the less-promising of the two was set aside. \"Basically, we decided to design an optical system that had two advantages over normal front projection,\" Dykstra continued. \"One was that it have an apparent projection stop aperture that was smaller than the taking aperture - which is one way to make front projection work beautifully, to get rid of the fringing problems. The second aspect of it was to use a light source that was primarily blue light in the range we wanted for matting -to get a very narrow band width light source and, as a result, make a bluescreen projection that takes advantage of the gain of front projection material. In other words, a very small light source giving you a very bright return. \"The Blue Max has some peculiar characteristics. For one thing, it seems to produce very clean mattes. When you put wires in front of the Blue Max, they tend to disappear - much more so than wires do in front of a frontlit or transmission screen. You don't have nearly as much light bouncing around all over the place, either. Another nice thing is that because the machine itself has a motion control capability and the projector and camera are tied together, if you make a front projection screen all the way around the room, you can do a 360-degree pan -which is something that wouldn't be practical with a frontlit blue screen. The Blue Max takes its blue with it, so anywhere you put a front projection screen you get blue. But the Blue Max has limitations, too. It's primarily for use in environments where you have actors relatively far away from the projection source. It doesn't work in closeup, because front projection doesn't work in closeup. You start getting inside the The heighliner cockpitoriginally a high-tech structure fashioned during Apogee's involvement - was altered significantly by the Smithies unit when David Lynch expressed his desire that it be given a more gothic look. Used primarily in the preparation of background plates for the third-stage navigator's spacefolding scenes, the final cockpit miniature was constructed from wood and fiberglass, and then filmed in a dense smoke environment for maximum aerial perspective. / The modular structure affords Brian Smithies little room as he squeezes in to line up a motion control shot. Photographing the cockpit interior required enormous amounts of light to illuminate the absorbent flat black surfaces. / Ken Worringham checks the density of dry ice fog moments before shooting. Recording the navigator's point of view, the camera was to rise out of what was intended to be a dense cloud of spice gas - a concept that was later abandoned. CINEFEX 21 • 45"],["46","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Inside the heighliner cockpit is a large translucent block of matter - a universe in microcosm, which the Guild navigators are able to mold and manipulate, and in the universe and the position of space-folding process, large plexiglass tanks were filled mixture of mineral oil and plastic shavings resembling plankton. Low-frequency undulating ripple effect. / Rambaldi assistant Bruno Landis blows dry a patched hole in the underside of the eighteen-inch creature - with eight cable-controlled points of movement - was filmed against a blue screen and used exclusively for the space-folding sequence. / Richard Malzahn completes the effect by animating the white light spewn from the process alter the real their ship within it. To suggest this nebulous with an organic-looking water, laced with small sound waves were introduced to create an small navigator. The navigator's mouth. 46 4 CINEFEX 21 hyperfocal distance of the lens, and people start to fringe because the projector can't carry depth of field. So if you need to do closeups of people, you have to use transmission blue.\" With Apogee no longer directly involved in Dune, Raffaella De Laurentiis remobilized the effects production effort with a call to Barry Nolan of Van der Veer Photo Effects. \"I had just returned from Tahiti after working on The Bounty when Raffaella called and asked me if I would fly down the next day to Mexico City and talk about Dune,\" Nolan recalled. \"So I did, and we spent the whole day discussing what the problems were and formulating a way of solving them. Much of the preliminary он на іс у об. work had already been done by Apogee. They had designed the storyboards and laid out the sequences. But the De Laurentiis people really had no idea of how to pull the various elements together, or how the special effects were going to be done. So we took the storyboards that were still in the picture - a lot of them had been cut out because of cost -and made a careful study of the project. Some of the deleted shots we brought back in; some we redesigned, introducing new elements. Altogether, we spent about three months planning what models would be needed, what stages would be needed, how long it would take to shoot and so on -and then broke each scene down into the assorted elements that would be necessary to finally put it together. iOne of the things we had to face immediately was whether to go on doing our work in VistaVision format, as Apogee had started to do, or to shift over into 4-perf, which I prefer. We were set up to go either way, but since only about ten percent of the work had already been done in VistaVision, I decided to go to 4-perf. We also had to decide on an approach for the traveling matte work. Normally, you can go to separations - using red, blue and green records - or to another method where you go to a half-blue exposure and a half-green exposure to come up with a synthetic blue that ultimately gives you a little more faithful color rendition. But there's another technique - what we call the 'color difference system' - which produces a matte through a combination of the green record and an excellent synthetic blue mask. The matte is actually black-and-white, but it represents the tonality and density difference between the green and blue separation positives. This color difference method really helps in faithful color reproduction, since almost everything pops out except the blues and purples; and so we decided it would be the best approach to use on Dune.\" Only one major area of concern remained. \"Apogee had offered the production a full service of miniature construction, motion control, opticals, storyboards, bluescreen work and everything,\" Nolan continued. \"We were able to supply the motion control systems, opticals and storyboard people, but we were not really geared up to handle model construction on the scale that they wanted and needed.\" In response to this problem, Raffaella De Laurentiis decided to enlist a separate model and miniatures crew, choosing as unit supervisor, Brian Smithies, an English technician who had worked with Les Bowie on Superman and a number of James Bond films, and independently as director of miniature effects on The Dark Crystal. \"Coming in midway through Dune was quite an experience,\" recalled Smithies, \"because the first few weeks were spent trying to find suitable equipment and setting up the shop and trying to find a crew, particularly a local one. But eventually I put together some very good people - like Peter Bohanna, who was our model shop supervisor; Rafael Ablanque and Christine Overs, our sculptors; Gus Ramsden, John Hatt and Rodney Fuller, our effects technicians; and Danielle Verse, a very good modelmaker. It was quite frantic at the beginning, actually, and we were always pressed for time; but given the fact that we were asked to do six months' worth of work in about a third of that time, I feel we all came out the other side rather heroically.\" The heroic efforts of Smithies and crew are best evidenced by the transformation of Masters/Lynch/Miller designs into a veritable armada of flying objects. One of the film's earliest scenes features a finished rendition of the Guild ship used to transport the third-stage navigator and his entourage. \"In the beginning, the design of the Guild ship looked something like a cross between a waffle iron and the hood ornament of a 1950s Pontiac,\" commented Ron Miller. \"Eventually it became very simplified, a kind of slab with a lump on one end and little bumps and contours, hardly any detailing at all.\" Made of fiberglass, he Sula ship r the Guild ship model was built on site and measured four by three feet. The cockpit was lit by small pea bulbs, with power supplied via lines running into the body of the ship and concealed by the selected camera angle. Mounted on a small camera crane, the ship was then lowered into one of the many slots which had been cut into a miniature representation of the Emperor's landing field, designed by art director Benjamin Fernandez. \"It took Benjamin and his crew of about forty a week-and-ahalf to build that set,\" commented Peter Bohanna. \"It was a giant tabletop set, about four feet off the floor, and since it was one of the last miniature sets to be filmed, the city you see in the background was actually bits and pieces of another miniature set - Giedi Prime city - which we'd built in modular sections. We simply took portions of that set and rearranged them for the city behind the landing field.\" The city modules were sculpted at the Churubusco model shop by Christine Overs and Rafael Ablanque. Silicone molds were taken from the sculptures, and solid polyester castings were then pulled from the molds. When the shapes and silhouettes representing the Emperor's city were erected on Stage 6, the structures were redone by hand with gold paint and placed on the wood and plaster tabletop landing field before a black cyclorama approximately thirty feet high and ten feet long. Lighting the set was accomplished by placing pea bulbs and normal low-wattage electric bulbs in various sections of the modules. A simple hand-controlled rheostat positioned outside camera range controlled light intensities. The finished set - sprawling over half the entire soundstage -"],["47","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 47"],["48","The Shape of ‘Dune’","was then illuminated to suggest a purplish twilight, and shot with the descending miniature Guild ship at 75 frames per The final shot in the arrival sequence - showing the navigators actually exiting the Guild ship - demonstrated the skill and expertise of Dune's foreground miniature expert, Emilio Ruiz del Rio. Ruiz, one of the leading world-class modelmakers, has provided miniatures for Italian, Spanish, German and American films since 1941, with credits on such productions as Doctor Zhivago, Solomon and Sheba and Nicholas and Alexandra, as well as De Laurentiis' own Conan the Barbarian and Conan the Destroyer. \"Ruiz' hanging models also seemed to be the answer to a certain realism problem in Dune,\" said David Lynch. \"When you do a clever in-the-camera special effects shot like the kind Emilio is so good at, then you can really sell that shot. Besides, some of these old-timey effects things are still just as viable today, even though a lot of people seem to have forgotten them.\" David and Raffaella were both concerned about adding a sense of bigness to the movie, to make it an epic,\" said Ruiz. \"So I and my people built a dozen models for the film. These were hanging miniatures and the more normal kinds of models, which took us nearly a year to build. The materials used for the construction of the models were primarily wood, plaster and fiberglass. The hanging models were very elaborate, especially the spaceships, because they were wired for lights and also had little ducts and hoses that let us blow smoke and steam through them. And though we did shoot some models at high camera speeds, almost all of the hanging miniatures were shot normally at 24 frames per second.\" Using a technique and location that would be repeated often during Dune's principal photography, Ruiz and Freddie Francis set up the Guild ship hanging miniature shot by placing their camera on a thirty-foot high platform situated in the car park of Mexico City's Azteca Stadium - one of the largest parking lots in the world. The under-section of the Guild ship model - which measured thirty-five feet by twenty-five feet and also incorporated part of a miniature landing field - was then hung three feet in front of the camera on a steel crossbar. The actors portraying the leather-clad first-stage Guild navigators were then stationed 250 feet away on a full-scale section of the Guild ship that included a landing ramp and some vertical panels. By using a deep-focus lens to hold a constant depth of field, the foreground miniature, background full-scale set and actors were all blended together, without the need for postproduction opticals. Also unique in design and concept were the Atreides spaceships. First revealed in scenes on the planet Caladan where Duke Leto and his supporters are preparing to leave their homeworld for the barren, dune-covered planet of Arrakis, the Atreides vessels conveyed a certain harmonious simplicity. \"The design of the Atreides spaceships had an interesting origin,\" Tony Masters explained. \"I was working at home one day, pulling out 48 • CINEFEX 21"],["49","The Shape of ‘Dune’","pieces of an old Victrola radio, and in the middle of it was a condenser wound in copper wire with these two strange ears on either side of it. And I thought: 'This is a hell of a good shape for a spaceship. I will use it one day.' And I did.\" \"We built twenty-eight separate models of the Atreides ships,\" said Brian Smithies. \"Ten of them averaged about three feet across, and they were the ones we usually set up for our bluescreen and motion control work. The smaller Atreides ships were twelve inches in width. Both sizes were made of fiberglass, pulled from silicone molds. Silicone rubber molds were used because we literally couldn't afford the time to finish up models generated from other types of molds that are less detailed. In fact, we didn't have time to take any chances with anything on Dune. Everything had to be right the first time. Basically, for all our models, we started off from a master that was made from any one of a variety of different materials - glass, plaster, material ors, pl wood, plastic or, very often, a composite of all of them. Then, from the masters, we took a silicone mold; and from the molds, we made our fiberglass replicas or breakaway glass replicas.\" \"To light the Atreides ship models,\" said electronic unit chief John Hatt, \"we had a small bulb at the end of each wing tip, and then a hundred watt quartz bulb in the center of the body covered by a bit of gel to diffuse the light. I would have liked to have put quartz bulbs in the wing tips, too, but we were working in a restricted space and they would have produced too much heat. Even as it was, the main lighting problem on the Atreides ship models was heat. We were doing ten-minute camera passes on them, and too much heat would have melted them - or produced smoke.\" The Atreides ship models share the limelight with yet another model shop creation in the scene where Duke Leto and his fleet enter into orbit and approach the huge cylindrical Guild heighliner for transport to Arrakis. \"The heighliner is basically just a cigar tube in space,\" commented Smithies. \"Our prime heighliner model was a plastic tube six feet long and nine inches in diameter - an EMA tube that was originally assembled by Apogee and which our little shop later refinished. During shooting, the heighliner was supported on a pole arm in front of a black backing. I decided against bluescreen for that ship because we didn't want the blue drifting up on the surface-which was fairly smooth and light-colored - and giving us a bad matte. So we shot that with black-and-white mattes, through which we could get a clean final matte. But that ship was only one element among a concert of them, for this scene had to be one of the most element-heavy in Dune. Not only was the Guild heighliner in the shot, but there were star plates and the planet and the moon of Caladan, too. And of course, you had all those Atreides ships streaming in through the heighliner door. For this one shot, Jimmy Devis and Ken Worringham shot some of the large Atreides ships supported on blue pylons in front of a blue screen, and we then multiplied those ships with different passes and different positions. The Atreides ships you see in the distance - the very small ones moving into the heighlinerwere actually cutouts tracked by Van der Veer's modified motion control unit. We had a number of small Atreides ship photos cut out, trimmed and mounted on clear glass plates, and then placed in front of the camera with the real models in the foreground. The effect was quite striking; it seems as though hundreds of those things are streaming in through the heighliner doors.\" \"Due to the pinched time frame we were working in,\" said James Devis, photographic supervisor for the model unit, \"we had a number of simultaneous setups going on Stage 2. While we were setting up one shot, for example, I'd try to light another setup off to the side somewhere. Or someone would be painting a model, or Brian would be getting a matte shot ready. You could walk into Stage 2 at any time and see three or four separate shots all around you, all getting ready to go. It was the only way to keep a jump ahead of everything.\" Motion control programmer Eric Swenson concurred. \"Things were really hectic. When Apogee bailed out and all of us from Van der Veer were hired on, we had only four months to shoot all the models. Plus, when we first set up at Churubusco, we were forced to begin filming right away with our motion control unit, without even having time to install a computer interface. So all the calculations for the various moves had to first be figured out by myself using a little hand-held calculator. Then I'd feed the equations into the stepping motors manually, with one indexer powering each motor. From that, we knew how many steps each shot should go. It was a prehistoric way to work; and it took us forever to get a shot. \"Our motion control rig had five basic points of movement, although when we first started using it without the computer interface, we were forced to make do with only three - pan, tilt and track. We also had two model movers we could hook up to it. And one of the nicest things we could put on the unitespecially nice for a big film like Dune- was a snorkel system that used Nikon lenses and could hold focus up to a quarter-ofan-inch. We could get really close to our models that way and get incredible wide-angle shots. That snorkel system went even wider than a standard anamorphic lens. It also had an anamorphic rear element, and Ken Worringham or Jimmy Devis could even rack focus on a model if they wanted to. The motion control system also had a high-torque motor on it for dollying back and forth, with a four-to-one gear on the rack. Normally we used about twenty or thirty feet of track for our shots. A one-inch diameter worm gear, connected to another motor, gave us our elevation: and we could pull 360 degree rotational shots if we wanted, too.\" Van der Veer's renovated motion control system was also able to accommodate the computer interface that freed Swenson from his laborious hands-on programming chores by utilizing a hybrid Zenith PC - refined by Barry Nolan - with 64K internal memory and two eight-inch floppy disk drives. Nolan and Swenson had, in essence, not only designed the entire unit, but its accompanying electronics package as well. A pensive Albert Whitlock with his painting of the Arrakeen valley - complete with monasterial cliffside castle, encroaching urban industrial area and gigantic shield wall in the background. Although he supervised each of the Dune matte shots, this was the only one personally executed by Whitlock. / Various elements were added to enliven the shot - an Atreides ship model photographed as three separate bluescreen elements, shifting cloud and shadow formations on the palatial fortress and a wispy superimposed smoke layer. CINEFEX 21 • 49"],["50","The Shape of ‘Dune’","responsible for numerous which added immense scale and production value to the film. For the arrival of the Atreides fleet at Arrakeen, Are des stop a ret stigh and thirty feet long - on a platform-mounted, forcedperspective landing field set smaller ships, a section of the shield wall and more than a thousand miniature formation. A portion of the accessway which descends from the body of the ship landing field. / Then, several hundred feet away, a full-size which live actors were able to pass. By carefully aligning the camera - equipped with a deep focus lens - the fullsize background set piece was made to blend perfectly World-class modelmaker Emilio Ruiz del Rio was foreground miniatures Ruiz supervised the that incorporated eight soldiers standing in was cut away, as was a trough-like strip in the representation of the missing accessway was constructed - through into the much more expansive foreground miniature. 50 • CINEFEX 21 Most of the motion control in Dune was shot using two cameras - the favored one being a Mitchell Standard modified by the Fries Company of North Hollywood. Both cameras were rented from Joe Dunton in London, who also supplied a video previewing system using three black-and-white monitors and two 3⁄4-inch video recorders. \"Obviously, we could see what the Fries camera saw,\" commented Brian Smithies, \"or see a film clip that we'd put in the camera. But the unique feature of Joe's system is its digital freeze-frame capability. It's like holding a clip in video, and you have absolutely no scan problem at all. It's a perfectly clean picture. By putting another image in on top of that digitally held frame, we could, in effect, comp up our shots on one video monitor. In other words, we could do video passes and immediately see what our elements were going to look like composited together, right there on stage.\" \"Another help during model photography was a stop-frame motor built by Ron Bickers,\" added effects cameraman Ken Worringham. \"The Fries motors - which are excellent and were put on both cameras - unfortunately could only go down to four frames per second. But with Bickers' motor, which we put on the Fries camera, you could go all the way down to one frame per second. So obviously, that gave us the ability to shoot in low light levels -and there's a lot of that on Dune. However, the Fries motors did certainly give us the ability to shoot high speeds, up to 120 frames per second, and we shot many of our smoke passes at that rate.\" An unanticipated, but essential step in the photographic process was a thorough, last-minute aerosol spraying of the motion control stage prior to each shot. \"Flies proved to be one of the biggest problems we had during the Churubusco model photography,\" affirmed Brian Smithies. \"It must have been the season or something. When you're filming a miniature and a fly the size of a jumbo jet scoots by at 5000 frames per second, it definitely presents problems.\" A major adjunct to the miniature ship photography involved the preparation of background plates for the scenes in which the floating third-stage Guild navigator folds space within the heighliner's cockpit -an interior structure which resembled, more than anything else, a vast, industrialized cathedral. Though a prototype of the cockpit had been fashioned in high-tech, horizontal style by the Apogee effects team, Smithies and his crew had to reaccomplish it, almost from scratch. \"David's concept of it was more vertical and antiquated,\" Smithies affirmed, \"so we had to completely readapt and change all the dressing to give it a gothic look. And that cockpit was supposed to be huge! The scale on it had to be large enough, in fact, to make the big third-stage Guild navigator you saw at the beginning of the film look like a tadpole floating around in it.\" The final miniature - painted flat black and filmed in a dense smoke environment for maximum aerial perspective - was ultimately composed of five ten-foot-high by fifteen-foot-long wood and fiberglass sections. The navigator featured in the cockpit sequence was the second of Rambaldi's third-stage models -an eighteen-inch version composed of an aluminum inner skeleton, polyurethane core and foam latex skin, and with only eight points of movement. The model was suspended on a horizontal steel pipe protruding through an eight-by ten-foot blue backing and photographed at Churubusco by Ken Worringham. \"Of course, the whole point of Carlo's second little navigator was to show how these strange creatures could fold space,\" commented Tony Masters. \"Inside the heighliner cockpit is a mini-universe - almost a jelly substance, which is pliable. By molding this substance, the navigators can, in effect, not only change the universe but alter the position of their own ship within it. Throughout all of preproduction and most of the principal photography, we were going to shoot this using David's original concept, which had the navigator actually ripping through this mini-universe with his hands, tearing a hole straight through to Arrakis by grabbing fistfuls of the jelly. But by the time we actually got to filming it, the concept had changed a bit.\" Changed, in fact, to the point where the navigator now spat a brilliant beam of molten white light at the mini-universe, in the process creating not only small representations of Caladan and Arrakis, but also a pulsating light bridge between the two. \"It's not my favorite sequence,\" commented Barry Nolan. \"Nobody really knew what David wanted. We tried boarding it and putting it together, but the concept of folding space changed, the colors and look of the navigator changed, and we had to search to try and find the concepts David wanted.\" Ultimately, Nolan and associates attacked the space-folding dilemma by building large plexiglass boxes and lighting them so that the outer edges looked like crystal. \"We shot it all motion control. Within the box was a very organic spaceDavid once described it as a giant jellyfish, complete with fluidity and undulation. For the inside imagery, we built backlit starfields that were placed beneath large tanks of water and mineral oil. We then put speakers inside the tanks, and introduced very low frequency sine waves which turned these containers into ripple tanks - creating movement in the mineral oil and water as it passed over the starfields. We built up layer upon layer of these undulated stars, and then introduced plastic shavings into the fluid that looked like plankton moving around in the eddies and currents - all to give it the organic look David wanted. The planets were three-dimensional models and the light the navigator spewed out was animated. All through this process, David kept changing the diffusion and other things, in an attempt to make it very nebulous-looking. It was one of the last things in the picture to be done, and unfortunately, it never quite came together. We just ran out of time.\" Lynch's gothic approach to the navigator's space-folding environment was also apparent in earlier scenes of the Atreides ships streaming into the much larger craft through its ornate, massive entryway - another large-scale miniature set. \"David wanted the door to the heighliner to look very imperial,\" said , \"so around the entrance is a very intricate bas-relief Smithies,"],["51","The Shape of ‘Dune’","depicting the mythology of that Empire - battle scenes from the early days of conquest, old emperors and the like. All of that ornamentation was done by Christine Overs. The panels around the door were plasticine. The ornamentation itself was done right on the clay sculpture, with bits of lace, paper doilies, plasticine carvings, and all manner of little knickknacks and cheap bits of jewelry. Then it was cast in fiberglass and spraypainted gold. The detailing on that paneling was very impressive - Christine even had the British Raj riding on the back of an elephant in there. Using the most mundane of materials, with great flair and imagination, she turned an earlier concept - which involved plate or etched glass and would have looked very tame and boring - into one filled with magic and imperial grandeur.\" Once inside the heighliner, the Atreides ships are seen being secured at the docking platform, a six-foot by five-foot fiberglass structure built by Gus Ramsden and equipped with a half-inch motorized rod attached to the back of a twelveinch ship model. Shot at high speeds, the rod retracted the model into its docking position. After voyaging to Arrakis in the Guild heighliner, the Atreides ships touch down on the Arrakeen landing field, making way for yet another of Emilio Ruiz del Rio's hanging miniatures. \"For this shot, we had an Atreides ship model - thirty feet long by ten feet high- in the foreground, and eight other six- by three-foot Atreides ships in the background. There was also a wood and plaster section of the shield wall that stood behind all this, thirteen feet high and nineteen feet long. Surrounding the big foreground ship were about 1300 miniature soldiers, about a third of an inch high. And supporting all this was a miniature airfield, painted with white landing zones, that measured fiftyfour by thirty-six feet. This whole big miniature - the ships and the soldiers and everything - was then put up on a platform thirty-six feet in the air, where we also put our camera. To make the shot work, we had to combine our miniature ships and landing field with real soldiers and the real actors. So on a parking lot at Churubusco, a set about forty feet high was constructed which was meant to be the lower part of the big foreground Atreides ship model. This full-size set had a door, stairs and big columns on either side that were supposed to be part of the ship's engines. We put the actors on and around the stairs, set up our miniature set and camera platform about three hundred feet away, and then tilted the camera so that the real set was framed in between the lower, cut-out portion of our miniature. That one shot took weeks to build and set up. Even so, it was an inexpensive way to get the effect. The Atreides arrival on Dune is now very big, very grand. And it is almost all done with miniatures.\" Duke Leto and his cohorts exit their ship in a flood of bright light and heat shimmer, and immediately enter the mountain fortress of Arrakeen - another Ruiz creation involving a thirtyby fourteen-foot hanging miniature positioned about ten feet away from the camera. Through a cutout section of the Arrakeen model, the camera was able to record the contingent of liveCINEFEX 21 • 51"],["52","The Shape of ‘Dune’","52 • CINEFEY 21"],["53","The Shape of ‘Dune’","action Atreides soldiers - actually some three hundred feet away - walking up full-size stairs that ended with some double doors at the top. Distant shots of the fortress were obtained solely with miniatures. \"The entire city of Arrakeen is based on Tibetan architecture - Tibetan monastaries, to be exact,\" Ron Miller noted. \"It also looks a little like the Matterhorn at Disneyland.\" Built entirely as a miniature on the Churubusco parking lot, the Arrakeen model was a five-sided construct built of wood, plaster and concrete, twenty-five feet across, ninety feet around and twenty-five feet high. Arranged with the mountain and palace in the center, a wall running around the perimeter of the mountain and a stretch of desert beyond, this very large miniature was supported on a wooden platform built three feet off the ground. Although used throughout the film for establishing shots, the Arrakeen miniature was built specifically to be destroyed - detonated by Kit West and filmed at 240 frames per second - during the Harkonnen's surprise aerial attack on the Atreides stronghold. A similar full miniature was used earlier in the film to represent the medieval-style Castle Caladan, perched atop a stony cliff and overlooking the sea. Though the sky above was a piece of stock footage inserted optically by Van der Veer, the remainder of the shot - castle, cliff and ocean below - were all part of yet another miniature created by Emilio Ruiz on a backlot at Churubusco. \"A miniature pool, about seventy-five feet wide and ten feet deep, was built outside near one of the soundstages,\" said Ruiz. \"Once it was filled, Kit West attached some small outboard motors on its edge to agitate the water. The rock wall was about five feet high, the castle four. Both were made out of wood and plaster. Then a small blue screen - about four by eight feet - was put in place behind the castle. We shot the whole thing at high-speed - about 72 frames per second.\" Compared to the relative simplicity of the Arrakeen and Caladan establishing shots, the effects-laden Giedi Prime sequence required a truly all-out effort by the Dune effects crews. Although the Harkonnen home base was featured in only a half-dozen shots, they were among the most visually intricate in the film; and as always, David Lynch had very specific design concepts in mind. \"Everyone has commented on how Victorian Giedi Prime looks,\" said Ron Miller. \"Well, they should, because it was intentional. David was quite clear in what he wanted on that planet, which had only one large city and was covered in seas of oil. He sent Tony Masters out looking for source material, and eventually Masters came up with some illustrations by a late 19th Century illustrator named Wegner. Much of Giedi Prime is based on that man's work, which looks heavily industrialized.\" The first shot of Giedi Prime showcases the spice processing Face Burner - built in the stylized form of the Baron Vladimir Harkonnen's visage - which uses burning oil to refine melange. A large model shot at Churubusco, the Face Burner was first sculpted in clay by Rafael Ablanque, and then covered with a plaster waste mold. The mold was then taken off the sculpture with the resulting model being cast in fiberglass and painted a steely, metallic gray. \"Most of the building and detailing on the Face Burner was done by Mario Gasca,\" said Peter Bohanna. iMario detailed the back of the Burner with large tubes made by Engineering Model Associates. EMA makes good things, and they have an amazing range of model sections. Their stuff is normally used for oil rig models or architectural models, so their specs are extremely accurate and demanding. I don't know what the Dune model shop would have done without them. \"The other detailing on the Face Burner was the result of my wife Jane's picking up some boxed model bridges in England - in other words, kit-bashing. We pulled a lot of little crossbeams and girders out of those kits and liberally applied them to our model. Really, we had a hard job on the Face Burner, because when we first detailed it we had put on bigger panels and bigger rivets. But when David Lynch saw a test shot, he wanted both the panels and rivets to be smaller. So we had to go back and scratch them all off and do it over.\" The completed Face Burner measured five-and-a-half feet long and approximately three-and-a-half feet high, and was equipped with practical lighting and an inner oil-burning smoke machine fed by a tube through the model's mouth. \"That's how we were able to get those billowing clouds that you see pouring out of the Burner in that first shot of it in the film,\" added Bohanna. \"David really likes things dark, so we laid on the smoke as much as we could. In fact, it got so dark in there on Stage 2 that we were literally crawling back and forth to the stage door on our hands and knees.\" \"We shot a lot of our models in a smoke environment,\" said Barry Nolan. \"One of my favorite films of all time is Blade Runner. I love the atmosphere in that movie, and we tried a bit to emulate that look in Dune. Of course, they were dealing with a smog background while we were dealing with sand, but the basic approach was pretty much the same. Unlike Blade Runner, however, where they used a frontlight/backlight matting technique, we used a bluescreen technique with multiple passes. First we'd do the shot with no smoke in the room, so we could pull a clean matte; then we'd go back and shoot it again with the smoke atmosphere - which was mineral oil smoke. Then combine the two. So it was sort of a dual-pass bluescreen technique. At the time, David wasn't really sure what he wanted, so we used a bunch of different techniques. Then, during the optical period, we went to him with these various approaches and let him choose what he wanted.\" Matching the effects photography with Freddie Francis' live-action work was often a problem. \"Although the Lightflex punches up the look of Dune, it actually gave some of us in postproduction a headache. Originally Freddie had wanted even the bluescreen shots to be done with the Lightflex, but that never really happened. So what we were faced with was effects footage that didn't match the live-action. Eventually we came up with a system of flashing our film in the lab that gave the effects a subtle sort of graying which helped lock them in The mountaintop Arrakeen palace was another Emilio Ruiz miniature - a largescale structure, twenty-five feet high and ninety feet around, built from wood, plaster and concrete in one of the Churubusco parking lots. / Attired in stillsuits designed by Bob Ringwood, Doctor Liet Kynes (Max Von Sydow) greets Paul Atreides (Kyle MacLachlan) upon his arrival at Arrakeen. The characteristic Fremen blue eyes - the result of prolonged exposure to melange - proved a major postproduction headache for the Van der Veer animation department. To produce the more than twenty-five minutes of 'blue eye' material required, some 136,000 orbs had to be rotoscoped from live-action plates, inked and colored by hand, and then matted back into the appropriate areas with just the right degree of intensity. / Rebecca Bush inks a pair of eyes into a medium shot. Longer shots often required that the work be done under magnification. CINEFEX 21 • 53"],["54","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Duke Leto and his entourage enter a full-size mockup of primary in-flight shots were Effects crew members Tom landing gear in preparation for bluescreen photography Verse adds finishing detail on two smaller ornithopters, Brian Smithies does a quick small-scale ornithopters on a camera crew prepares for a shot of the 'thopter traveling Subsequently, it was decided that the ship was too large animated shadow completed an Atreides ornithopter. / The vessel's takeoff and obtained with a fullyarticulated miniature constructed by Apogee. Valentine and John Hatt adjust the collapsible of the takeoff. / Danielle used for distant shots. / on-set touch-up of the ornithopter's underside. / John Hatt rigs one of the wire while the effects over the shield wall. in the frame, so it was reshot as a bluescreen element and matted into some static frames at the start of the shot. An the illusion. 54 4 CINEFEX 21 with the rest of the film.\" Due in part to the reduced light levels caused by the dense smoke environment on Stage 2, it took a considerable amount of time to record the multiple passes required for the initial Face Burner shot. \"David wanted this long, slow track-in on the processor,\" said Bohanna. \"Each time, the camera had to move in only about four feet, but the motion control unit needed about ten separate twelve-minute passes to do it.\" As difficult as it proved to be, the Face Burner was only one component among many in the murky Giedi Prime sequence. Another was a model of the city itself - an industrialized maze of girders and cabling which resembled a futuristic oil refinery. The twelve- by sixteenfoot modular structure - pieced together from dozens of smaller panels cast in polyester from silicone molds - was sculpted by Gus Ramsden, with painting and detailing done by Ramsden and Danielle Verse. The fabrication and final assembly took five weeks to complete, and the model was illuminated with 150 pea-bulbs scattered throughout the structure and with greengelled spotlights casting light from behind. The Giedi Prime model was positioned in front of a small blue screen for a shot in which the city, the smoking Face Burner and various lights were composited simultaneously, along with an additional miniature - a small cable car. Although the cable car which actually stops within Giedi Prime was a full-size prop, a much smaller one was used in the establishing composite. \"I built a cable car about eighteen inches long by six inches high,\" Bohanna explained, \"which took about three weeks of work. It was made out of wooden patterns, glass moldings, EMA plexiglass sections and a tiny bit of widgeting, or kit-bashing. Inside, there was a wood and cloth doll, about four inches high, that my little boy made. It was an amazing job, very lifelike.\" The miniature cable car, lit by 400 watts of incandescent bulbs, ran along a thirty-foot-long aluminum girder three inches thick. An electrically-powered winch, pulley and counterweight, mounted on the camera-blind side of the car, provided the actual movement. \"We shot the cable car against a blue screen, as a separate element,\" said British traveling matte expert Stanley Sayer. Sayer, whose career began in Technicolor's titles department, and who spent forty years with that company, is a major figure in the international bluescreen community. With an extensive body of work in such landmark effects films as Raiders of the Lost Ark and all three Star Wars episodes, Sayer would ultimately oversee approximately 150 bluescreen shots for Dune. \"Most of Dune's bluescreen work was done using four different kinds of screens. One was a very large front projection screen that we used for all our backgrounds during the worm riding shots. Another was a standard canvas screen painted aquamarine blue -which, of course, is best lit by arc lights covered with deep blue filters. But on Dune, I had to use Dino's own particular brand of lights - which are basically incandes- - and by the time I put those deep blue filters on them, I hadn't much light left. So for most of the bluescreen and model photography using this standard canvas, I was forced to really"],["55","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 55 MOST POPULAR IN EARTH WILL BE"],["56","The Shape of ‘Dune’","56 • CINEFEX 21 VAN DER VEER BIDE PARKING OALY"],["57","The Shape of ‘Dune’","increase my lighting levels. I also used a blue velvet-type screen on Dune, which is very handy because you can roll it up and put it in the back of your car and just stretch it out when you need it again. But the blue velvet has a disadvantage in that it's almost translucent. As a result, the light seeps through it, and you lose a lot of illumination that way. My favorite way of doing bluescreen work is with the Stewart screen - which is a rear projection screen, dyed blue and backlit by fluorescents. The nicest thing about the Stewart screen is that you get no spill from it, and I was able to use this particular screen for many of the model shots in Dune. \"The Giedi Prime shot, however, required a very low angle, looking up at it. So in order to have the blue screen at right angles to the access of the lens, I had to hoist a twenty- by fifteen-foot canvas screen up nearly to the ceiling of Stage 2 and then lay it forward at a 45-degree angle. The cable car model was then hung in front of that screen by means of a fifteen-foot-high scaffolding that supported a wooden plank running above the length of the aluminum girder which supported the car. The girder itself hung down from the supporting planks by thin metal rods which had to be rotoscoped out of the shot.\" The rotoscoping and final assembly of the shot were to take place months later in Van der Veer's in-house animation and optical departments. \"By the time we had finished compositing all the separate elements in that Giedi Prime shot,\" said optical coordinator Patt Harrison, \"the cable car support was so tiny in relation to the city and Face Burner that we had to sweeten the aluminum beam holding up the car with animation by adding a thin black line to its path of travel. But that was just a small example of what we did on Dune. At Van der Veer, we have two double-head printers, two single-head printers, Barry Nolan's modified motion control unit, an Acme animation stand and three rotoscope stands. We also do our own black-and-white processing for mattes. And we used virtually every piece of equipment that we have on this film.\" Meanwhile, a few miles down the road at Universal Studios, another element of the Giedi Prime sequence - the cable car's passage over a maze-like view of the city - was being rendered under the watchful eye of veteran matte shot innovator Albert J. Whitlock. Although Whitlock had made a number of trips to Mexico for consultation, and had returned to Churubusco with his matte cameraman Bill Taylor for the live-action plate photography, the paintings themselves were executed and photographed at Universal. As is customary with the Whitlock technique, every effort was made to have each shot structured in such a way that the painting would be photographed on original negative, without the consequent image degradation inherent in duping processes. Also, whenever possible, moving elements and/or camera moves were incorporated into the shots to help obviate the essentially static nature of the paintings. \"The Giedi Prime shot was unusual for several reasons,\" said Bill Taylor. \"First, because there was no set involved at all. The set had long been struck by the time we got the assignment. So it's a full painting, with just a couple of live-action inserts. Second, the painting was actually begun before the live-action elements were shot. Third, it also marked the first time we photographed our own motion control miniature- the cable car - for incorporation into a matte shot.\" Working from a sketch provided by Tony Masters, matte artist Syd Dutton was given liberal freedom to interpret the Giedi Prime setting, \"I really enjoyed doing that,\" Dutton said. \"My original idea was that it would be something like an old-fashioned train station, and at one point the painting even had a vaulted ceiling overhead with rib-like iron beams. But then David came in and looked at it and said he wanted it to have more grime and more cables, and eventually it was changed to better fit his concept.\" The final painting, which incorporated two groups of uniformed men, as well as the cable car entering below camera and passing overhead, also featured a tilt-up to follow the cable car into the distance. The painting itself was on two planes, with the foreground cables painted on a front glass. When photographed, a motorized cam mechanism repeated the tilt on successive exposures. To obtain reflections similar to that which might be found on a metal floor, the live-action extras were photographed in a wetted-down parking lot, with the camera shooting down from an eighty-foot platform. To insure that the perspectives matched, a frame clip of the painting was positioned in the camera viewfinder. Reduced and repositioned separations were then printed from the original live-action negative, with an optical tilt that matched the tilt on the painting frame-byframe. A similar approach was employed to incorporate distant jets of steam and fire, which helped to sweeten the shot. A foot-long miniature cable car - backed by a blue screen - was then mounted on a twenty-five-foot track and pulled away from the camera. The camera also tilted in scale to keep painting and miniature locked together. Various window mattes insured that the actors, smoke and fire would be cut off in the proper places. For the final composite, the painting was photographed in three passes - painting, foreground wires and diffused lamp glows - with repeated tilts on each pass. As the painting was shot, a film matte for the cable car ran in bi-pack. Then the cable car, the uniformed men and the smoke and fire were all added on the optical printer - requiring another eighteen passes. Though the Giedi Prime matte shot was the showcase for Whitlock's department, other shots in the film employed similar enlivening techniques - including two shots of the Arrakeen valley, one painted by Syd Dutton and the other by the master himself. \"Al's painting of the ships coming into the Arrakeen valley was pretty complex,\" said Bill Taylor. \"Since there was only one model, we photographed it in three different bluescreen passes using a crude motion control system from Universal's old Hartland facility. Some of the moves, like a roll, were done with just a hand crank. A smoke layer was shot in miniature against black velvet, with the smoke floating on a Camera operator Gordon Hayman films an ornithopter POV as the flying craft descends on a spice harvester in distress. The large-scale harvestersculpted by Rafael Ablanque and cast in fiberglass - was positioned on a miniature desert setting constructed outdoors at Churubusco. / Earlier approach shots were obtained on the interior worm stage using a smaller version of the harvester. / During the final postproduction phase at Van der Veer, a small sand crawler - actually a prototype toy incorporated for its merchandising potential - was photographed scooting up a rear ramp into the harvester. CINEFEX 21 • 57"],["58","The Shape of ‘Dune’","A monstrous set of worm abandoned spice harvester. / Between shots, model shop supervisor Peter Bohanna constructed from beeswax to facilitate its being crushed by the giant sandworm. / For mechanical worm head was elevated miniature desert system enabled it to thrust mouth functions. / As the worm breaches, high-speed cameras record the action. / Preparing for a shot of the Verse spreads colored 'spice' MacLachlan. Back in Mexico dubbing session, the young actor pitched in on some jaws close about the examines the diminutive spice harvester - these shots, Carlo Rambaldi's twelve-foot positioned beneath the setting. A counterweight upwards, while cable operators controlled the large harvester, Danielle particles on the sand. Assisting her is Kyle for a postproduction of the effects work then in progress. 58 < CINEFEX 21 boundary between cold and warm air. An additional smoke plume was simulated with a moving cotton shape. Both smoke elements were combined into a color matte film. In order to get convincing cloud shadows passing over the painted palace, a forced-perspective foam core mockup of the palace, the mountain and the shield wall was photographed with several different-speed shadow patterns passing over it, and this footage was then composited over the painting. The combined shadow, smoke and ship mattes ran in various combinations- both bi-pack and tri-pack - when the painting and artwork overlays were being photographed, and another bi-pack pass printed the burn-in exposure on the smoke layer. Finally, twelve more passes on the optical printer were required to add the bluescreen ships.\" A similar technique was used for the more distant Arrakeen valley shot, with the added employment of moving cotton shapes to simulate massive storm clouds visible over the top of the shield wall. Paul's appearance before the Fremen multitudes in the Hall of Rites provided yet another opportunity for the Universal matte department to take a fairly simple setting and give it an enormous sense of scale. Live-action elements for the sequence were photographed at night in a long, narrow set constructed on one of the Churubusco backlots. \"The original side walls of the set were twenty feet high,\" Bill Taylor recalled, \"and the end wall was about eighty feet high. We added to all those walls in our mattes, which, for the high angle shots, were filmed with VistaVision and a 2-to-1 squeeze along the short dimension. The camera was turned sideways so that the long dimension of the frame was vertical, and we put some mattes in front of the camera to block out the areas we didn't want. Most of the crowd in the distance is painted, as is the ceiling. The final painting was photographed on original negative, with an optical tilt made later on CRI (color reversal intermediate) - a standard Al Whitlock technique for incorporating a zoom or pan and tilt.\" In all, three paintings were rendered for the Hall of Rites - each a collaboration between Syd Dutton and Albert Whitlock's son, Mark Whitlock was also responsible for the final matte shot in the film - a distant view of the Arrakeen palace as Paul miraculously brings rain to the ever-parched planet of Arrakis. \"That shot is a full painting,\" said Taylor. \"We had a live-action plate of Fremen extras in a crowd, but it just didn't work out. What you see of the crowd is all painting -with certain areas scratched away and a moving backlit grid behind to create a bit of animation. We shot miniature cotton clouds in multiple passes, strongly underlit with orange- and red-gelled lights. We took separation masters of those, and then split-screened them onto the sky, which was also painted. The moving searchlights and rays of light breaking through the clouds are all added exposures from artwork. Then the rain was superimposed in on top.\" \"Al is really bold with his concepts,\" commented Dutton, \"and he's not afraid to take out a tremendous amount of a shot"],["59","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 59"],["60","The Shape of ‘Dune’","60 • CINFFEY 21"],["61","The Shape of ‘Dune’","and then paint it back in, rather than darning and mending, which is kind of an old matte technique. And he'll try to get an effect - a light change or moving clouds - anything that will make the painting look real. I think this is the reason why recognition came so late to Al Whitlock. A lot of his early work wasn't in 'special effects' movies, and so nobody was looking for matte shots. Instead, they were all just accepted as reality. His work was so good, nobody noticed it.\" The mile-long sandworms of Arrakis - a basic, yet extremely vital element of the Dune story - were featured prominently throughout the film. Like the intricate Giedi Prime sequence, the multi-effect worm shots brought together the diverse skills of many talented effects masters. Most of the shots were filmed indoors on a giant 'worm set' constructed on Churubusco's Stage 4. One hundred feet wide, sixty feet deep and built up on platforms five feet off the floor, the worm set was shaped in a gigantic half-circle and sectionalized so that portions of the set could be detached and reassembled during filming for maximum versatility. Behind the giant tabletop was a curving thirtyfoot-high sky cyclorama painted by Ted Mitchell- an English scenic artist who had previously worked on Raiders of the Lost Ark and Return of the Jedi. \"Until we got very near to the end of the picture,\" said Barry Nolan, \"the exact color of the sky really hadn't been determined. As a result, Ted had to repaint that cyclorama four times before we got the daytime sky look David wanted.\" Many of the scenes had to be enhanced during the optical postproduction period. \"In the final battle, especially,\" explained Patt Harrison, \"you see a lot of dust blowing around in the frame that's been optically introduced into the shots. We'd take a piece of negative with blowing sand and make a lowcontrast print, then combine that with an interpositive of the background. So basically, we were printing black density sand onto the background of a lot of those shots.\" Working closely with effects coordinator Chuck Finance, Nolan was to spend three months supervising the actual worm shoot - a total of some 125 shots. Before any of that, however, he first had to lay out the sequences, determine camera angles, select appropriate lenses and worm sizes, and design a workable worm set. \"Each of the table sections was first covered with sawdust and cloth to create the basic contours and shapes of the dunes. Then about five truckloads of sand were brought in from Samalayuca - an unusual, beige-color sand that was very pretty - and that was spread out all over the table and then textured and scalloped to look like real sand dunes.\" The only area that wasn't covered in sand was the 'worm table' -a twenty-footsquare portion of the set into which tracks and runners and other facilities needed for the actual worm effects had been installed. For that key area, something finer in scale than real sand would be needed. \"When we were trying to plot out the best way to give absolute realism to our worms,\" said Kit West, \"shooting them at high speeds seemed an obvious part of the solution. High speeds would give them a sense of scale and bulk. But what about the sand on the desert around them? I knew right away that we were going to have trouble using regular sand because the grains would be too big against the worms, and David really wanted these worms to flow through the sand, to look like they were swimming through water. So, instead of sand, we used a mixture of fuller's earth and microballoons. We used tons of fuller's earth on this film, not only on the miniature desert shots, but to blow around in the air to simulate dust storms on the live-action sets as well. Fuller's earth is a very, very fine powder, like talc, that is actually mined. It has a slightly brownish tinge and no smell; it's not dangerous to the eyes; and it won't even really hurt you if you swallow mouthfuls of it. All we did was lighten or darken it slightly, by putting a tint of color to match whatever background we had. \"Along with the fuller's earth, we used 5000 pounds of microballoons on the worm set. Microballoons themselves are incredibly small, hollow spheres of borosilicate glass that are about 2.5 thousandths of an inch or 60 microns across. The microballoons worked very well. They were so small in relation to the worms that they flowed like water off them. But microballoons aren't as safe as fuller's earth. In fact, the damn things are very dangerous; once they get in your lungs they stay there. So all of us on the worm set always had to wear face masks to filter out the air.\" Dune's microballoons were spread around the detachable section of the worm table with small compressed air hoses which the crew used to blow the dust and balloons off and onto the worms and miniature landscape. Small hand sieves were also used to sift the mixture over the set and worms. The task of compelling the sandworms to flow through the deserts of Dune fell to Carlo Rambaldi and his crew of sixty worm handlers -all hired locally in Mexico City. Some were Mexican, some were American, some were French and German. Even a few tourists were recruited. The only firm prerequisite for the job was that they speak either English or Italian. \"While the handlers were responsible for the more complicated moves,\" said Nolan, \"the gross movements were created by mounting the worms on wooden plywood tracks under the sand. The tracks - about half-an-inch thick - were shaped like sine waves to give the worms their desired wiggling motion, and a variable speed 50 horsepower electric motor on each track pulled the creatures forward.\" \"To operate three different worms at the same time,\" elaborated Rambaldi, \"took nine people at the worm controls. But to operate the largest worms took eighteen people. Most of the worms were connected on the bottoms of their bodies to three or four different points on the tracks. When we first started to film the worms, security was very tight and people had to wear ID badges just to get on the stage. (Despite such precautions, one of Rambaldi's worms was stolen during filming.) Even so, things could get crowded in there. Both Brian Smithies and Barry Nolan had a crew of thirty-five people, and my crew was on stage all the time. But things were funny, too. There were a lot of On stage, Brian Smithies examines the Harkonnen flagship - spearhead of the aerial armada launched against Arrakeen in an allout surprise attack. / Flies were a constant problem on the Churubusco soundstages, necessitating a thorough insecticide spraying before any miniature shot could even be attempted. Here, Smithies and James Devis perform the duty in preparation for a bluescreen pass on a portion of the Harkonnen fleet. / A massive pyrotechnic assault on the palace was engineered by mechanical effects supervisor Kit West and shot at high-speed. CINEFEX 21 • 61"],["62","The Shape of ‘Dune’","For two of Paul's psychic visions, a foam rubber fetal representation of his sister actuated mouth, neck and limbs - was fashioned in the likeness of Alicia Roanne playing Alia. Carlo Rambaldi examines his handiwork in the company of Witt's real mother, who was recruited birthing sequence which was ultimately deleted from the final cut. / A Harkonnen ornithopter carrying Paul and Jessica into the deep desert crashes en route. As part of the sequence, the 'thopter model was mounted in front of a blue screen and suspended from the ceiling via bunjy cords - readily Worringham to incorporate a rough, bouncing movement camera crew during a setup flagship perched atop its 'steel tent' - a nine-story portable palace that unfolds - complete with cableWitt, the young actress to play a midwife in the filmed with a camera allowing operator Ken into the shot. / Brian Smithies addresses the involving the Emperor's from the ship. 62 CINEFEX 21 signs around, painted in Spanish, that said 'Don't Step Herel' or 'No Photos!' And one of Nolan's people made this big hand fan out of white cloth to wave Kit West's smoke around - the kind of fan you see slaves waving over the pharaoh in movies, but this one had 'Eat at Joe's' painted on it.\" Kit West's 'smoke' was a unique and noxious solution to a requirement that David Lynch had added to the worm shoot. \"David had very definite ideas about the texture and coloration of the smoke surrounding the worms seen in the later battle scenes,\" said Freddie Francis. \"He wanted blacksmoke, and the usual methods just weren't dark enough for him. So Kit West came up with this horrible mixture that did the trick. Of course, we'll probably all die of black lung, as a result.\" \"What I did,\" explained West, \"was cut up real rubber tires and then sprinkle in napthalene powder on top. When this burned, it gave the smoke an extra blackness. We used thousands of cut-up tires all through Dune - some for the miniature work and some for the live-action battles. Pretty foul stuff, really. We had to wrap our high-speed Photosonics camera's magazine -the one we were using on Stage 4-in a green plastic trash bag to protect it. We put this burning mixture in steel drums, contained inside small wire cages to help trap the larger bits of burning rubber and stop them from floating around in the air. To push that smoke around, we had a wind machine on the worm set- a very large one. It was basically just an old aircraft engine with a propeller on it. We used six of those wind machines on Dune, mostly for the outdoor storm sequences. But 1 gather, from the reaction of the crew, that that smoke inside Stage 4 was pretty foul.\" \"Foul indeed,\" agreed Francis. \"The stages at Churubusco were not the most modern. And the ventilation was awful. After we filled those stages with smoke, we'd do a couple of takes and just open all the doors and wait an hour for the stage to clear. Extractor fans really didn't exist in Mexico. We shot the worm set with Kodak's new 5394 stock because we wanted a little bit of extra speed for the worm stuff, which we were shooting at ten to twelve times normal speed with our Photosonics camera. This high-speed camera was capable of going up to about 360 frames per second - which is generally what we used on the worms. Our lighting requirements for the worm set were based on all this high-speed photography, so it was really litl Besides the studio's own power source, we had to line seven portable generators up outside the stage doors just to power all these lights. We must have been using about 11,000 amps of power. Of course, this meant that it became very hot in there - well over 100 degrees.\" Many of the shots obtained on the worm set would appear in the film without significant embellishment. Others would become plates or bluescreen elements for composite shots with live-action performers. \"For scenes where people are seen running alongside the worms,\" said Nolan, \"we did a lot of very long shots of real people running out in the Samalayuca desert. Then we matted them in with our miniature worms. In one scene, where a worm pushes sand over the Sardaukar troops, we just piled some sand and microballoons on the edge of a big horizontal blue screen, and then had a worm enter the frame and push it down onto the screen. We matted that into a live-action plate shot from an eighty-foot-tall platform we'd erected on the back lot for POV shots of the worm riders. We had to build that tower - a tubular steel structure - around a tree, of all things, because it was the only location we could use to get that height. We didn t want to cut the tree down, so we just built around it. It was like the biggest treehouse in the world.\" Not all of the worm shots were filmed exclusively on the worm set. One in particular, featuring a worm attack on a spice harvester as seen from a hovering ornithopter, was shot outside in the sunlight -an approach John Dykstra planned to use throughout, had he remained on the project. From an aerial perspective, the heaving bulk of the creature is first seen approaching the harvester beneath the sands - in actuality, one of Rambaldi's small worm models wrapped in a plastic sleeve and pulled beneath the Stage 4 set, an effect later enhanced with flashing static discharges provided by the Van der Veer animation department. In an attempt to warn the harvester crew, Duke Leto has a frenzied radio conversation with one of the technicians below - a cameo appearance by David Lynch, filmed in Los Angeles by cinematographer Jack Cardiff during Dune's optical postproduction phase. As the Artreides ornithopter approaches the harvester to rescue its crew, several long shots and closeups of the mobile spice-mining machine are featured. \"That was our large eightfoot-long harvester,\" said Brian Smithies. \"We made two of them, one for sectional explosions during the montage of the Fremen exploding all the harvesters, and another for the background plates and closeups. Actually, these two models were the ones that required the most work; they had a great deal of finishing detail on them. The big harvesters were first sculpted in plaster by Rafael Ablanque, then cast up in fiberglass from silcone molds. Before we dirtied them down, they had an interesting coloration - sort of a cast iron blackish-gray. They were then transported to a large exterior set that we had out in the parking lot-another miniature desert set, about forty feet across and twenty feet deep, that we covered in sand and microballoons tinted with a red dye. To get them to move, we simply put them on tracks buried beneath the sand, equipped with a simple motorized arrangement. Behind the set, we had another eight- by twenty-foot cyclorama for the sky of Arrakis that was a lightish-tan color. This was where we blew up one of our large harvesters - over and over again, a panel here, a panel there.\" Also featured in the harvester destruction sequence was a wedge-shaped carryall - the airborne transport used to ferry harvesters across Dune's surface. \"We built one large carryall model out of fiberglass, with breakaway panels, and used it for an early shot in Paul's filmbook and then later when it's blown up by the Fremen in the desert. It was three feet long by one foot high, and was carrying a little harvester about five-and-"],["63","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 63"],["64","The Shape of ‘Dune’","64 4 CINEFEX 21"],["65","The Shape of ‘Dune’","a-half inches long. We blew it up in the Churubusco parking lot - just mounted it on a pole and used two cameras shooting at 120 frames per second.\" As the thopter lifts off with the harvester crew, the harvester itself begins to shake as the oncoming worm draws near - movements accomplished manually by technicians positioned underneath the elevated platform set. Moments later, the massive head of a sandworm appears beneath the harvester and swallows it. \"That was another harvester entirely,\" explained modelmaker Danielle Verse, \"a model made out of beeswax, fifty centimeters long and equipped with small explosive charges. The worm we used was Carlo's twelve-foot mechanical head. We had to build another set for this shot out in the parking lot. It was only a big platform, really, about twenty feet high and twelve feet across - with a hole in the center for the worm. First, we covered that hole with sand and put our little harvester on it. Peter Bohanna and I were up above, using a fan and some fuller's earth to kick the sand around a little. Then, underneath, Carlo's crew had the worm head attached to a counterweight and running on a steel track. They dropped the weight, the head pushed up through the hole and then its mouth closed around the model.\" Later in the film, Paul must participate in the most dangerous test of Fremen manhood -riding a sandworm. \"I knew the worm ride was one of the little hearts of the book,\" said David Lynch. \"And I knew we'd better come up with something good for the film, or we'd be in big trouble.\" Paul's ride began on location in the Samalayuca desert, where two hundred workers from the Dune unit spent two months clearing a three-squaremile patch of sand of all vegetation and other signs of life. Scenes were then filmed of Paul planting a thumper and calling a worm, intercut with shots of Rambaldi's worm head rising out of the miniature worm set sand at 240 frames per second. A bluescreen shot of Paul in the foreground and one of Rambaldi's large worm models in the background completed the worm ride \"When you see Paul running alongside the worm, just before he plants his maker hook, we're then actually on the backlot of Churubusco,\" explained Kit West. \"We built a false worm section fifty feet long and eighteen feet high out of wood and rubber - and that had a very slight curve to it. We then hung this section on a tubular steel framework equipped with ten axles and twenty wheels. This moved along a 200-foot railway-type track which we had on an endless wire propelled by a four-wheel drive jeep. We just drove the jeep up and down the backlot, the section moved and Kyle ran alongside it. We'd also mounted some air blowers on the railway that were hidden by the mounds of sand we'd trucked in. When we turned these blowers on, it kicked all that sand up into the air and gave a pretty good impression of the worm plowing through the desert.\" Paul then plunges his maker hook into the side of the worm, exposing a glistening, tendon-filled inner section. Composed of latex and gelatin, the inner segment was detailed with hundreds of condoms whose tips had been removed, cut lengthwise in half, and then laboriously applied by hand to the section. With its tender inner flesh exposed to the gritty sand of Arrakis, the sandworm instinctively rotates away from the irritation, rolling on its side and pulling Paul up to the 'top' of its body. \"This was another reason why we designed the worms to have those three-lobed mouths,\" noted Tony Masters. \"No matter how they rolled around, they always looked like they were still upright, so to speak.\" To suggest the upward rotation of the worm, Kyle MacLachlan was posed on a worm skin section in front of a blue screen, and the camera itself then moved down towards the stationary actor. The worm segment itself never moved. \"All the shots where anyone's standing up on top of the worms were filmed indoors on Stage 2, on what I called the worm platform,\" said Kit West. \"Basically, the platform itself was forty feet square and built out of wood, sponge rubber and a lot of detailed, hand-painted latex that stood in for worm skin. It was a big thing, filling up two-thirds of the stage. But I was very happy with the support and rocker system we built underneath this platform. What it was, essentially, was a hollow half-sphere made out of hundreds of thin metal strips-eight feet in diameter and four feet deep. The wide part of the ball supported the platform; the curved part was sort of nestled in this square socket supported by these huge truck suspension springs that allowed the entire unit to be rotated very easily. In fact, we counterbalanced it on all four corners and in its front and back so well that four people could easily grab a corner and just walk the whole thing in a full circle. And it weighed tons! The fact that it rotated so easily came in very handy. We could do full-on or over-the-shoulder shots without ever having to move the camera or change our lighting setups.\" West also detailed the skin of the worm platform with four blowholes - orifices through which the sandworms expel sand accidentally taken in through the mouth. Pneumatic compressor tubes were installed for each of the blows one end of which led to a hopper filled with fine sand and the other terminating in a ring device which opened and closed off the tubes. The rings were further overlaid with latex. On cue, the latex pulled back, the tubes opened, and air pressure blew out a shower of sand particles. After Paul has securely fastened himself to the top of the worm, the other Fremen race alongside the beast, tiny dots in relation to the worm's huge bulk. Joined by his Fremen friends, Paul triumphantly rides the worm across a sea of sand. \"During Paul's ride,\" said Stanley Sayer, \"you have a miniature worm head rearing up in the foreground, the actors standing in front of a blue screen in the midground, and a miniature worm tail and desert in the background -a very complex bluescreen shot. That sequence was about the only thing in Dune that was shot using Apogee's Blue Max system, although originally, we were going to use it for much more. To this end, Apogee built for us the biggest Scotchlite front projection screen I've ever used - 108 feet wide and 55 feet high. Unfortunately, it burned Paul Atreides - now Muad Dib - addresses the Fremen multitudes in the Hall of Rites. The partial live-action set - an exterior erected on the Churubusco backlot - was a narrow, open-roofed structure two hundred feet long, with an eighty-foot elevated platform at one end and ten-foot walls along the sides. The balance of the structure was achieved in matte painting form by Albert Whitlock's unit. / Syd Dutton - who worked on the paintings with Mark Whitlock - adds a finishing touch to one of the angles. / David Lynch - on a platform next to the Blue Max front projection system - supervises bluescreen plate photography of actors Everett McGill, Kyle MacLachlan and Patrick Stewart during the worm ride sequence. CINEFEX 21 • 65"],["66","The Shape of ‘Dune’","Electrician Ruben Huerta and modelmaker Rafael de Maria wedge-shaped carryall for a fleetingly in the film - was transport unit for ferrying equipment into spice-rich areas of the Arrakis deserts. / Barry Nolan lines up a shot Carlo Rambaldi's twelve-foot worm head. In addition to corresponding tail section, y Campos prepare the bluescreen shot. The carryall - shown only designed as an aerial harvesters and heavy into the gaping mouth of the closeup head and a Rambaldi and his unit produced five fullyarticulated worms - in fifteen- and twenty-twofoot lengths - plus ten nonarticulated threefoot versions. 66 4 CINEFEX 21 down before we were able to use it for anything other than the worm ride. \"It happened one day when we were setting up a fairly elaborate shot on stage - a desert scene where, in the background, worms were rearing up and Sardaukar soldiers were running away from them. All of a sudden, an overhead lamp burst and the hot filament fell onto one of the flags we used to prevent light from bouncing off the high-gain front projection screen. The flag, which was fabric, caught fire and it spread to the screen. The flames just raced along the cover at the top - and the screen itself actually became molten and began to run. Of course, in came the fire brigade with their hoses and their powder and they ruined the whole screen. All told, we had $50,000 worth of damage. And that stage had to be cleared for 5000 on. so de another film, so we couldn't even get back in there. As a result, we were forced to come up with another solution, and I must say that what we came up with was better than our original ideal\" The 'better idea' was a sunlit, outdoor blue screen - unconventional by contemporary standards, but a staple technique in the past. \"Our backlot blue screen was about two hundred feet long,\" said Barry Nolan, \"oriented east and west and tilted at a 45-degree angle, so that for about four hours of the day, we had optimum light accumulation on it. Five feet in front of the screen, our desert set was built - about ten feet off the ground, with ladders and stairways in the back so our extras could run up and over the tops of the dunes. It's not the most ideal way to do bluescreen, but it can be made to work. Ultimately we did about forty of our battle shots with this system.\" Stanley Sayer was more favorably impressed. \"I think it worked out very well. Originally, we were supposed to spend weeks on those shots, using the Blue Max. We got the same material outdoors in only three days!\" Postproduction on Dune was split between Mexico and the United States, with the ongoing worm and miniature effects being shot at Churubusco while optical and animation work was underway at the Van der Veer facility in Burbank. When the Churubusco shoot was finally completed, the production moved north to set up its editorial headquarters on the second floor of Van der Veer's studio - thus allowing frequent consultation and interaction between Lynch and Barry Nolan. Before leaving Mexico, however, it was decided that an expository introduction to the complex narrative would be needed - featuring the Princess Irulan against a glittering starfield -and so actress Virginia Madsen was recalled to duty. \"We decided to open the film with Irulan,\" explained Nolan, \"because in the book, the action comes from her diary. So we brought Virginia back to Mexico and shot that introductory segment with her. It was photographed on black. Later we generated the starfield and rotoscoped her into it. People ask, Why does she fade in and out?' Because they cut segments out of her speech. The whole thing had been done as one continuous take, and fading was the only way we could hide the cuts. We just faded out, cut, and faded back in again.\" The starfields used in the prologue, and throughout the film, were produced and packaged at the Van der Veer facility. \"For the graphics, all the starfields were painted by Ron Miller,\" said Nolan. \"But the reality starfields were kodalith backlit stars. When normal starfields are shot with anamorphic lenses, there are often aberrations in them, so we got around that by making anamorphic stars and then shooting them flat. We began by computer-generating the stars as little dots on white paper - little 2-to-1 ovals. Then we pasted all these dots on a board and made large black-and-white kodaliths of them, which we backlit. We photographed them with a very good flat lens, and as a result, ended up with really sharp stars that were already in anamorphic format-but without any aberration. You can't rock or roll or turn on them, but then we didn't need to.\" Another element added to the film's opening during postproduction was 'A Special Report within the Guild'-a verbal dossier prepared by a member of the Spacing Guild discussing the primary worlds of Dune and the acquisition of melange. This segment - complete with images of Caladan, Giedi Prime, Arrakis and Kaitain - was inserted as an afterthought when early preview audiences indicated confusion as to what was going on. \"I had spent two years on the picture,\" said Nolan, \"and read the novel Dune and two of its sequels. So I knew the characters and story inside and out. This was a problem though, the same problem we all had. We knew everything so well, but outside people began telling us they had difficulty in understanding what was happening. So little things were added. We included a lot of voice-over narration, for instance. And the secret report at the beginning served to clarify the fact that we were dealing with four different planets, and to explain a little bit about each. We hoped to eliminate confusion over the difficult pronunciations, as well as help to define some of the weird vocabulary.\" Ultimately, all four planets featured in the secret report were done as paintings by Ron Miller, who produced them at Van der Veer in a single week's time after the crew returned from Mexico. The paintings were then photographed and put together optically, with a change in backgrounds giving them a three-dimensional look. \"David's really into hallucinatory imagery,\" said Nolan, \"and the film features a lot of dreams and visions and hallucinations - his 'little movies within movies,' as he called them - most of which we had to assemble in optical. David would create some beautiful things, but often they were difficult to understand and make work. Some of them didn't even make it into the film. For instance, there was a scene where Paul travels down into 'the place where no man has ever gone,' drinks the Water of Life - and lives. For his hallucination during that sequence, we built a huge vagina - an interior cavern that was about twenty feet long and covered with a mucosal material. We then built a snorkel lens system on the end of a twenty-foot dolly that could drive right down into it, built the lighting around the snorkel system and shot it at high-speed. It was really fantastic-looking"],["67","The Shape of ‘Dune’","-but it eventually worked its way out of the picture. People just didn't understand what was happening.\" Another recurring image was of an open hand. \"People kept wondering what that was all about,\" Nolan continued. \"Well, to begin with, that was my hand. David put in all kinds of little cameos and vocal appearances by people who had worked on the film. But what it was supposed to be was the hand of God - literally. That would have been more apparent had they kept in a scene that was dropped after the first preview. It was at the end of the film and this giant hand comes down out of the clouds over Arrakeen. A bolt of lightning shoots out of its palm, and it begins to rain. It was all supposed to be a symbol of Paul's powers.\" Other imagery was less obscure. Early in the film, as Paul sleeps in his Caladan bedchamber, he has a disturbing prophetic dream featuring a stunning black-and-white image of molten light shifting across the face of dark water - a high-speed shot filmed at Churubusco in a large, specially-built tank lined with black mylar. Paul next envisions the cataclysmic explosion of Arrakis' mouse moon - so named because of the mouse-shaped silhouette on its surface. \"For that shot,\" said Nolan, \"we used a number of solid plaster moons that we hung up outside, twenty feet in the air. The faces had been prescribed into chunks and then reassembled. We used a Photosonics high-speed camera - shooting into a mirror for safety -and we just blew them up. There were a lot of takes - mainly because what we wanted were flameless explosions, which wasn't easy.\" Requiring an even greater amount of production time - and considerably more effort - was Dune's personal body shield. First demonstrated in the knife fight between Paul Atreides and warmaster Gurney Halleck, the body shield is composed of impenetrable slabs of solid electricity - crystalline-like structures resembling a stylized suit of armor that is impervious to all but the slowest attempts to penetrate it. Although Apogee had shot motion control and bluescreen plates for the combative training sequence, Barry Nolan found that a considerable amount of reevaluation was in order. \"We talked and talked and talked with David about the concept,\" Nolan recalled. \"We did blow-up color prints of the people and sketched different shield ideas over them, trying to find a single-frame graphic representation of what David wanted in there. Then we did an animation test of a part of that scene, using a design David liked, burning a basic structure of the shield over the shot as it was. David really wanted a rigid-looking box, but it had to give a little. We went through this process until we developed the 3-D structure which moves around the body. Then we said, 'What are we going to put into it?' David's explanation was a continuously changing, distorted field of flux that moved around all the time as if it were electrically charged, and kept moving and shifting even if the people themselves were stationary.\" Richard Malzahn -head of Van der Veer's rotoscope and animation department - along with Jeffrey Burks, spent about nine months animating the elaborate sequence. Once the basic CINEFEX 21 • 67"],["68","The Shape of ‘Dune’","68 4 CINEFEX 21"],["69","The Shape of ‘Dune’","animation was complete, the shields were then broken down into specific surfaces, with each individual surface being treated as a separate element - a process which entailed the hiring of ten additional artists to assist with inking and painting. The end result was a separate animation cel for each and every shield \"To give it the 3-D look we wanted,\" said Nolan, \"we built a computer-controlled ripple wheel system on our optical printer. With that, we were able to create ripples and motion, and go back and be able to repeat them precisely. We called the surfaces 'facets' - like a diamond - and after careful calculation, each separate facet was optically treated with different sets of ripple glasses that would give us varying kinds of effects on different surfaces. Then we optically reinserted the original image back into each facet with this distortion. Essentially the same technique was used for the shield wall that rises around the Arrakeen palace - but in that case, with only one facet, there wasn't much character to it. On the body shield shots, David also wanted some of the images to have not only an optical distortion, but also kind of a satiny surface on some of the planes. So we invented a diffusion process for that also. We also changed the colors slightly on the sides, as compared to the front. And as it moved around the room, it picked up highlights, which we animated and laid in as little sheens that would kick off. Then, when the fields touched each other, there was a bit of electrical discharge and we'd alter the direction of the ripple wheel - make it go forward and backward, pause and move fast or slow down, and just generally create a continuous feeling of strangeness going on inside.\" Another unique aspect of the knife fight is the unearthly crackling sounds the shields emit as they touch and rub against one another. \"The shield sounds were composed of eight separate tracks that I treated with a flanger and equalizer,\" said sound designer Alan Splet. \"There were a lot of different sounds in there, including a low roar I pulled off of an ultra-sound cleaning machine, a buzzing, a noise from a heavy power cable that was arcing and sparking a lot, and a couple of other growls and low hums that I produced myself.\" A former accountant who left his then eight-year-old job to become a soundman for an industrial film company, Splet first met David Lynch in 1970. The two have since worked together on all of Lynch's films, shorts and features alike. \"David has always been as obsessed with sound as he is with imagery,\" said Splet. \"As a result, I was working on Dune even before the start of principal photography.\" Splet's contributions to Dune's sound included overseeing the film's sound editors, recording mixers and engineers, and working closely with associate sound editor Les Wiggens - as well as doing a remarkable job of collecting his own effects. \"By now, I've built up a library of about 200,000 sounds, some of which wound up in Dune. But I used a lot less of my library on Dune than I have in some other films. Most of Dune's sound effects were freshly recorded, because I like to go out and get fresh material whenever I can. Not counting the musical soundtrack, about eighty percent of the sounds of Dune were man-made. I think it's interesting, too, that about two-thirds of the effects were worked up before I ever saw the picture. I just based those sounds on my talks with David.\" The Bene Gesserit weirding module - a device which translates sound waves into a powerful destructive force - was a joint aural and optical effect. \"Our contribution was minimal,\" said Barry Nolan. \"The weirding devices basically produced just sound, so the animated blobs we put in were very vague, virtually on the edge of not being there at all. We did a lot more in the battle sequence. David didn't like the idea of doing traditional laser beam-type effects, so the Sardaukar had guns that shot out something like tracer bullets. We worked on various things, and even got some old World War Il Victory at Sea battle footage and analyzed the tracers in that. David liked the approach, but he wanted to have something happening within them, so our animated tracers have little squiggle-type things inside to give them a strange motion as they move across the screen. These were done by our four graphic arts people - each one basically laying the sequence out using charts to show what the tracers would look like.\" Of considerably greater consequence for the Van der Veer animation department were the Fremen blue eyes - a characteristic result of prolonged exposure to the pervasive effects of melange. \"The eyes were always a problem,\" said Raffaella De Laurentiis. \"They were so familiar to Dunereaders that we knew we'd have to deliver a very good effect throughout the picture.\" To this end, a number of different approaches were attempted - including tinted contact lenses, an organic eye dye and a computer animation technique. Clearly, either the contact lenses or dye would have been preferable, requiring no postproduction time. However, the scleral contact lenses required to render the entire eye blue proved uncomfortable - even hazardous in an environment where blowing sand and dust were the norm - while the blue eye dye proved even worse. De Laurentiis herself tried it, and was rendered sightless for two days. Digital Productions' computer animation technique, tested but not perfected, was dismissed early-on as being too costly. \"Finally,\" said Barry Nolan, \"we decided to go with rotoscoping. The first thing we had to do on each shot was develop a satisfactory eye matte. Whenever possible, we'd do high-contrast prints of the live-action and try to isolate the eye areas from the actual photography. More often than not, though, we had to do articulate rotoscope matting - an enormously painstaking job that fell to Katie Rauh in our animation department. Once we figured out how to build the matte itself, we then developed a technique for inserting it into the eye. If we'd just gone back and double-exposed it into the eye area, then the eyes would have glowed - which we didn't want. So, instead, we had to matte down the old eye and bring the density back up with the blue. In the very beginning, David and Raffaella just wanted the whites blue. Then they changed their minds and wanted a Most of the worm photography took place on a giant interior worm set comprised of multiple platforms that could be reconfigured and dressed to fit the varying needs of each individual shot. Although most of the set was covered with sand trucked in from the Samalayuca location, the central section - where most of the worm action was to take place - was covered with a much finer mixture of fuller's earth and microballoons. Barry Nolan and crew shoot a background plate of the tail section for later compositing with the live-action worm riders. / Nearly all of Rambaldi's sandworms were employed in a series of forced-perspective shots depicting the creatures' passage through a breach in the Arrakeen shield wall. / Surrounded by cable operators, Gordon Hayman shoots high-speed footage of a rearing sandworm. CINEFEX 21 • 69"],["70","The Shape of ‘Dune’","To avoid the inhalation of microballoons, filter masks were mandatory for all crew members working on the worm stage. / The closeup against a blue screen. / A Arrakis. Blowing sand and black smoke - from burning shots. / Even after the worm unit wrapped at Churubusco, an occasional insert would be deemed necessary. Here, a motion control camera on the Van der Veer effects stage is trucked toward the worm head - with moving smoke overhead suggesting stormy skies - for a battlefield POV of Sardaukar soldiers being creature. / For a scene of a worm diving into the sand, Barry Nolan employs one of worm heads, mounted on head is photographed giant sandworm plows through the deserts of rubber - were staple ingredients of the worm run over by the giant Rambaldi's smaller-scale the end of a rod. 70 * CINEFEX 21 powder blue white and a dark gun-metal blue iris - a totally blue eye. That wasn't easy. Again, if we went too far, the eyes would glow; and if we didn't go far enough, the eyes would just disappear. Each eye had to be wedged. And it wasn't anything we could evaluate on a light bench. We had screening rooms all over town that were balanced for color temperature and brightness so we could check our effect. If there was any sign of chatter or alignment problems, or if the color was moving around the eyes, we'd kick it out and do it over. Some of those eye shots were done four dozen times - most of them at least six-just to find the right visual combination. In all, there was a little more than twenty-five minutes of 'blue eye' material on the screen, with anywhere from one to twelve sets of eyes per frame. In the end, we figured out that we'd done about 136,000 eyes.\" After three-and-a-half years of intensive labor, the David Lynch vision of Dune was drawing to a final wrap. \"David was given carte blanche to make this film the way he wanted,\" Barry Nolan concluded. \"They literally never said no to him. Raffaella said: 'This is a David Lynch film and I want him to make it the way he wants. I'm not going to block him every time he says he wants to do something.' So we did whatever he wanted. During the last phase of postproduction, David had his office right upstairs from us, and we spent a year working very closely together. I must have seen The Elephant Man a dozen times and Eraserhead half a dozen times, just to clue in on what he was as a man. It was a beautiful working relationship - no fights, nobody got fired. The film ended up with 68 minutes of opticals - just a hair over half the total running time. All told, there were 550 optical shots -and of those, I'd say that 75 percent incorporated original photography. So it was a big job - for everyone concerned. And with very few exceptions, we all walked away proud of what we had done.\" Unfortunately, the phenomenal success of Frank Herbert's novel was not to be repeated in the first film based on his chronicles of Arrakis. In the wake of persistent rumors that Dune was in serious trouble, Universal released the film nationwide on December 14, 1984. Although it enjoyed a strong opening week, Dune quickly plummeted from the Christmas hit list and by February had grossed only $25 million - less than half its cost, and far short of the estimated $200 million needed to clear a profit. Despite the film's epic presence and inventive soundtrack, audiences simply found Dune too bewildering. Perhaps expecting the surface flash of Star Wars, they found instead a somber, complex attempt at serious science fiction. Yet for all its boxoffice shortcomings, Dune remains a dedicated and reverent attempt to bring an intelligent, adult speculative novel to the screen. \"No matter what happens ultimately to the film,\" concluded Frank Herbert, \"David and Raffaella and everyone else who worked on this picture really have given it their best shot. They have nothing to be ashamed of, and I'm totally satisfied. They've made the real Dune.\""],["71","The Shape of ‘Dune’","CINEFEX 21 • 71"],["72","Closing pages",""]]});
